02:17:56.530 --> 02:17:59.829
Good evening, Mayor, council members,

02:17:59.878 --> 02:18:03.442
and fellow residents. My name is Alex Washburn. Over the

02:18:03.942 --> 02:18:06.736
past week, I've spent dozens of hours reading the city's budgets,

02:18:06.896 --> 02:18:10.961
attending meetings, filing grandma requests, and trying to understand

02:18:10.977 --> 02:18:14.756
why Eagle Mountain is proposing what amounts to a 220% property

02:18:14.789 --> 02:18:18.543
tax increase. Yesterday, I had the opportunity to spend

02:18:19.043 --> 02:18:21.714
about an hour meeting with Mayor Jared Gray, the city manager,

02:18:22.035 --> 02:18:25.859
Council Member Clark, and members of the finance team. I sincerely

02:18:26.359 --> 02:18:29.553
appreciate each of them taking the time to answer my questions because

02:18:29.569 --> 02:18:32.686
that meeting genuinely changed my understanding of several things.

02:18:33.505 --> 02:18:38.228
I now have a much better understanding of how Utah's truth-in-tax law has

02:18:38.405 --> 02:18:42.164
affected Eagle Mountain over the last 15 years. As property taxes—

02:18:42.292 --> 02:18:45.746
values increased, the certified property tax rate decreased,

02:18:46.196 --> 02:18:49.648
while the cost of providing services, especially especially law enforcement,

02:18:49.665 --> 02:18:53.642
continued to rise. I now understand why the city has gradually

02:18:53.658 --> 02:18:57.171
had to use more and more of the general fund to cover the increasing

02:18:57.187 --> 02:19:00.940
cost of the sheriff's contract, leaving less capacity

02:19:00.972 --> 02:19:03.827
for roads, infrastructure, parks, and other priorities.

02:19:04.501 --> 02:19:07.468
I believe that's a real problem, and I believe it needs to be addressed.

02:19:07.709 --> 02:19:11.430
However, I still have 2 significant concerns. The first is the

02:19:11.462 --> 02:19:15.328
policy objective itself. During yesterday's meeting, it was explained

02:19:15.828 --> 02:19:19.420
that this recommendation was built around fully funding the sheriff's

02:19:19.920 --> 02:19:23.220
contract through property taxes. But when I reviewed the 2010 budget,

02:19:23.720 --> 02:19:27.020
the last time there's a tax increase, I found that property tax revenue was

02:19:27.520 --> 02:19:31.740
approximately $974,000 while police expenditures

02:19:32.240 --> 02:19:35.420
were approximately $2 million. So unless I'm misunderstanding those

02:19:35.920 --> 02:19:40.020
numbers, property taxes did not fully fund law enforcement even then.

02:19:40.160 --> 02:19:43.880
That tells me that fully funding the sheriff's contract through property taxes

02:19:44.380 --> 02:19:48.056
isn't simply restoring the way things have always in, it's a policy decision.

02:19:48.570 --> 02:19:52.232
And I think taxpayers deserve to understand why that policy was chosen.

02:19:52.907 --> 02:19:56.779
Why is it better to have property taxes fully fund the sheriff's contract

02:19:56.987 --> 02:20:00.698
instead of continuing to fund it through a combination of property taxes and

02:20:00.762 --> 02:20:04.875
other general fund revenues as the city has historically done? My second

02:20:05.375 --> 02:20:09.100
concern is how the amount of this increase was determined. During yesterday's meeting,

02:20:09.196 --> 02:20:12.409
I asked what I thought was a simple question. If this proposed

02:20:12.909 --> 02:20:16.277
tax increase were reduced didn't pass at all, what projects or

02:20:16.341 --> 02:20:20.094
priorities would actually be delayed or eliminated. The answer was

02:20:20.110 --> 02:20:23.782
that those decisions haven't been made yet. I appreciate the city

02:20:23.895 --> 02:20:26.990
genuinely wants public input before making those decisions,

02:20:27.407 --> 02:20:31.640
but I respectfully believe that the analysis should have come first. 30 seconds remaining.

02:20:32.618 --> 02:20:35.810
I wish the city had come to the public with several funding scenarios.

02:20:36.179 --> 02:20:39.594
If we approve the full increase, if we reduce it by 10%, or reduce by

02:20:40.094 --> 02:20:42.437
20%. What projects would have to wait?

02:20:43.194 --> 02:20:46.641
That would allow taxpayers to have an informed discussion about priorities

02:20:46.754 --> 02:20:49.427
instead of simply reacting. Based on everything,

02:20:50.016 --> 02:20:54.198
I support raising it to $1.5 million to fund the 2027

02:20:54.214 --> 02:20:58.170
sheriff's contract, but I oppose the increase beyond that unless the

02:20:58.670 --> 02:21:02.196
city can clearly explain exactly what the remaining dollars will fund

02:21:02.696 --> 02:21:06.847
and what projects or priorities— You can't talk—

