and, uh, any council, uh, questions or concerns.

03:06:51.741 --> 03:06:55.507
So, uh, I think I'm going to start on my right, which is

03:06:55.797 --> 03:06:58.853
Zach. Councilmember Dewish, if you want to address public— Yes, follow up.

03:07:00.752 --> 03:07:04.133
Do you have a timer for me? Okay. Would you

03:07:04.633 --> 03:07:06.549
like a timer? I can do a timer. I would like a timer, please.

03:07:07.016 --> 03:07:10.236
I've got more than 3 minutes. Okay, go ahead. But I'll keep this part

03:07:10.736 --> 03:07:13.475
to 3 minutes. First, I wanna say I'm sorry that you're here.

03:07:14.508 --> 03:07:18.173
I am grateful that you all showed up. For those that have come, the gentleman

03:07:18.205 --> 03:07:21.580
that was up at 2:30 this morning and got off work at 4:30 to be

03:07:22.080 --> 03:07:26.337
here, I'm sorry that you're here. Quite frankly,

03:07:27.169 --> 03:07:29.027
I don't want to be here. I don't want to be in this chair.

03:07:30.810 --> 03:07:34.531
I threw my hat in the ring for Mayor Gray's city council seat because we

03:07:35.031 --> 03:07:38.511
have had compounded serial missteps in our city for

03:07:39.011 --> 03:07:43.237
several years. I've lived here over 20 years, and quite frankly,

03:07:43.593 --> 03:07:46.315
we, we need to step it up as leaders in the city.

03:07:48.058 --> 03:07:51.713
I'm hearing 4 themes. One is affordability, which maybe

03:07:52.213 --> 03:07:55.569
I'll, I'll address a little bit later. Transparency— we

03:07:55.585 --> 03:07:59.586
failed you. We absolutely failed you. I took it into my own hands

03:07:59.650 --> 03:08:03.040
to, to do a podcast. It's hard to communicate effectively

03:08:03.540 --> 03:08:06.864
to a city of 80,000 people. The citizens page, I just didn't

03:08:06.880 --> 03:08:10.238
feel like was a good way to communicate in, in sound

03:08:10.334 --> 03:08:13.708
bites. There's been a lot of miscommunication. It's been

03:08:14.208 --> 03:08:17.484
told that this was passed unanimously. It was not passed

03:08:17.984 --> 03:08:21.594
unanimously. It was. Even though that's out on the citizens page, so I tried to

03:08:22.094 --> 03:08:25.175
put a podcast out at my own expense to try to build context.

03:08:25.271 --> 03:08:28.563
I've put my phone number out there. I've I've had

03:08:28.579 --> 03:08:32.320
five or six residents reach out to me personally. I've had ten to fifteen

03:08:32.416 --> 03:08:35.178
emails, and I'm just grateful that we are here tonight.

03:08:35.643 --> 03:08:38.790
Even though none of us wanted to be here, I'm grateful that we are here.

03:08:38.999 --> 03:08:42.146
This process allows everyone to share their thoughts, their feelings,

03:08:42.210 --> 03:08:45.211
and it's scary to come up. To the pulpit in front of the dais.

03:08:45.340 --> 03:08:48.612
I've stood there myself. The second thing

03:08:48.644 --> 03:08:51.997
is the policy, which we're going to come back to. I want to spend more

03:08:52.497 --> 03:08:55.992
time there. And then third, the data centers. That has been one

03:08:56.024 --> 03:09:00.821
of those compounding things. The state has floated,

03:09:00.901 --> 03:09:05.136
or policymakers at the state have floated, the idea of socializing

03:09:05.168 --> 03:09:08.810
the MET tax that we've all talked about, that we've all been counting on

03:09:08.858 --> 03:09:12.210
and told that we're going to have this tax. There is a

03:09:12.710 --> 03:09:16.011
policy that was not passed, was defeated,

03:09:16.476 --> 03:09:20.325
that would essentially take the municipal energy tax and socialize it to other

03:09:20.825 --> 03:09:23.660
cities. In my mind, that's wrong. We've been waiting.

03:09:24.174 --> 03:09:27.670
We've been, you know, promised that that tax would come back to our city.

03:09:28.183 --> 03:09:32.272
So there are very active, real threats around

03:09:32.772 --> 03:09:36.089
revenue to our city, and we're trying to manage that as best we can along

03:09:36.185 --> 03:09:39.562
with the, with the caps. I don't

03:09:40.062 --> 03:09:43.116
know that this is the right rate. Um, I didn't vote for

03:09:43.616 --> 03:09:47.295
this in the first round, and I'm, I'm hoping to have a lively discussion

03:09:47.554 --> 03:09:49.109
with everyone tonight. Thank you for being here.

03:09:51.423 --> 03:09:54.649
Councilmember Wood. Go ahead, start my timer.

03:09:56.383 --> 03:10:00.218
Thank you for speaking. I think everybody here that knows me or that

03:10:00.235 --> 03:10:03.934
has listened to me knows that We're not afraid to hear your words. We want

03:10:04.434 --> 03:10:07.932
to hear everything that you feel. And sometimes we get stuff wrong.

03:10:08.692 --> 03:10:11.246
I mean, that's just the reality. I would tell you the number one highlighted thing—

03:10:11.827 --> 03:10:14.559
I share your frustration. I think we did a horrible job of communication.

03:10:16.240 --> 03:10:19.893
I, I think that's been highlighted by every person here. And so

03:10:20.393 --> 03:10:23.756
for that, I want to apologize. And I don't remember who the gentleman was that

03:10:23.788 --> 03:10:26.397
said it was our responsibility to communicate that. I think it was one of the

03:10:26.642 --> 03:10:29.494
people online. I will own that.

03:10:30.047 --> 03:10:33.984
I should have communicated. To Zach's

03:10:34.484 --> 03:10:37.760
point, it is difficult to find a method that we can reach

03:10:38.260 --> 03:10:41.504
to people and get truthful information out. There is so much

03:10:41.536 --> 03:10:44.770
disinformation that is out there. It's in the media,

03:10:44.915 --> 03:10:48.876
the social media outlet. Go to the Eagle Mountain Citizens

03:10:48.924 --> 03:10:52.128
page. There's not— there's very little truth in anything that's being said in there.

03:10:53.273 --> 03:10:56.372
And I debated, how do I get the information

03:10:56.404 --> 03:11:00.819
to people? How do I tell them why I'm interested in voting for

03:11:01.319 --> 03:11:05.010
a tax increase of any size? We're gonna have a chance

03:11:05.510 --> 03:11:08.462
to talk about that, and I wanna address that at that point. But I agree

03:11:08.591 --> 03:11:11.641
that there are the 4 things that we need to talk about. What is real

03:11:12.141 --> 03:11:16.586
with data center money? What are the challenges that we're facing from the state in

03:11:17.100 --> 03:11:20.098
their efforts to try to raise additional revenue? Additional funds at our expense?

03:11:20.584 --> 03:11:23.740
What are some of the laws that have been floated that were defeated that

03:11:24.240 --> 03:11:28.142
we know are coming back? And then I think I definitely

03:11:28.158 --> 03:11:31.783
want to talk about the way that we communicate with you. I've been very frustrated

03:11:32.283 --> 03:11:34.393
with that long before I was ever on this council, and I don't think it's

03:11:34.893 --> 03:11:38.872
improved all that much, frankly. I think that the information

03:11:39.372 --> 03:11:42.272
that you expected, I would expect if I was in your seat, and we didn't

03:11:42.772 --> 03:11:46.376
provide that information to you. And I will address that in that latter part.

03:11:47.154 --> 03:11:50.201
Thank you for taking the time. It's a great sacrifice for you to be here.

03:11:51.825 --> 03:11:55.687
That's not lost on me. I appreciate you being here so we can actually have

03:11:56.187 --> 03:11:58.713
a dialogue and explain to you some of the things that we're thinking about and

03:11:58.745 --> 03:12:01.291
where I'm gonna land is not predetermined.

03:12:02.275 --> 03:12:04.516
I will tell you, I'm not sure where I'm gonna be at the end of

03:12:04.532 --> 03:12:08.095
this. We did have to make a decision to be able to

03:12:08.595 --> 03:12:12.103
have this discussion. So we started with what are our actual needs,

03:12:12.460 --> 03:12:14.991
and I'd like an opportunity to be able to explain that to you.

03:12:19.344 --> 03:12:22.862
Thank you. Councilmember Whiting. Thank you so much for

03:12:23.362 --> 03:12:26.653
being here. We really, really appreciate you. We appreciate all the comments.

03:12:27.376 --> 03:12:30.079
We appreciate you that you've stayed here already for over 3 hours,

03:12:30.578 --> 03:12:33.959
and we hope you can stay for the duration

03:12:34.459 --> 03:12:37.732
of the commentary here. There's been a lot of questions asked. There's a lot of

03:12:37.780 --> 03:12:39.929
information that's going to come back your direction.

03:12:41.420 --> 03:12:44.337
Skepticism of government is a highly healthy thing,

03:12:45.395 --> 03:12:49.724
and I encourage it. I applaud it, and I hope the Eagle Mountain City— the

03:12:50.224 --> 03:12:54.068
citizens here will ever remain skeptical of government, whether it's local

03:12:54.568 --> 03:12:58.396
or state or federal. We need to do that. We've had so much experience

03:12:58.637 --> 03:13:02.246
in the past where there has been fraud and abuse at the

03:13:02.311 --> 03:13:06.087
levels of government. We've experienced that even recently in the last couple

03:13:06.587 --> 03:13:10.891
weeks. For those paying attention to hearings

03:13:11.325 --> 03:13:15.149
in Washington, D.C., we have learned that the federal government

03:13:15.888 --> 03:13:19.134
was deceitful on numerous levels and numerous

03:13:19.634 --> 03:13:22.139
occasions with how it handled the COVID situation.

03:13:23.135 --> 03:13:26.477
We are learning that Dr. Fauci in his private diary was saying one

03:13:26.654 --> 03:13:30.669
thing and publicly saying something completely different. That affected public

03:13:30.685 --> 03:13:34.090
policy in a significant way. The social distancing was a lie.

03:13:34.299 --> 03:13:37.832
There is no science behind it. Dr. Fauci admits that. That's what

03:13:38.332 --> 03:13:40.947
shut down our businesses. That's what shut down our schools. That's what shut down our

03:13:41.447 --> 03:13:44.529
churches. And that is what led to massive inflation.

03:13:45.685 --> 03:13:48.881
That's what led to significant costs we are now experiencing.

03:13:49.716 --> 03:13:53.522
But I only bring that up because skepticism

03:13:53.554 --> 03:13:57.228
is healthy. You need to come before us and say, you guys need to document

03:13:57.260 --> 03:14:00.789
this, that, and the other. You have the right to know. You need to make

03:14:01.289 --> 03:14:03.804
sure that we're not doing the same thing that other government leaders have done.

03:14:04.141 --> 03:14:07.606
So I applaud that you're here. The only thing that's sad about it

03:14:08.106 --> 03:14:11.937
is we only have a few of you here. There are so many online that

03:14:12.017 --> 03:14:14.984
will get their information from social media where there are only half-truths,

03:14:15.466 --> 03:14:18.353
or people think they have the truth and lead a whole garrison of people the

03:14:18.853 --> 03:14:21.866
wrong direction. So I hope you'll stay so we can have a dialogue that can

03:14:22.363 --> 03:14:26.201
that can be healthy, where we can transmit information as to why would we consider

03:14:26.701 --> 03:14:30.151
an increase? Why would we do something that would be unpopular? Why would we want

03:14:30.651 --> 03:14:33.924
to do that? It would be easy for any of us to

03:14:34.424 --> 03:14:38.340
be the single champion and have a whole— people rally behind us.

03:14:39.544 --> 03:14:41.952
Why would we consider the tax increase? So I hope you'll stay so we can

03:14:42.452 --> 03:14:45.469
have additional discussion, and I really appreciate again that you're here. Thank you.

03:14:47.674 --> 03:14:50.862
Okay, thank you. Councilmember Wright, go ahead. Thank you, Mayor.

03:14:52.557 --> 03:14:53.545
It's been good to hear from you.

03:14:55.920 --> 03:14:59.248
Um, somebody said that we're going to take the easy way out. I can assure

03:14:59.748 --> 03:15:02.685
you this is not the easy way out. Um,

03:15:05.699 --> 03:15:09.069
the easy way out is really ignoring the needs that the city is experiencing,

03:15:10.180 --> 03:15:12.530
the growth that we're, that we're, that we're dealing with.

03:15:13.868 --> 03:15:17.530
The loss of employees because we can't keep

03:15:17.579 --> 03:15:20.317
them even in a median area as far as payroll goes.

03:15:25.830 --> 03:15:28.895
There's a lot that we need to discuss, but I do want to thank you

03:15:29.395 --> 03:15:32.680
for coming. I appreciate you sharing your thoughts. I really

03:15:32.745 --> 03:15:36.316
hope you'll listen to the discussion that's going to ensue because I

03:15:36.816 --> 03:15:39.761
don't get the feeling, I haven't had the feeling that that this is settled in

03:15:39.874 --> 03:15:43.102
an exact place, but we're going to have some

03:15:43.602 --> 03:15:47.394
dialogue and some discussion about it. But I am

03:15:47.427 --> 03:15:50.851
absolutely considering a tax increase

03:15:51.351 --> 03:15:54.818
for all of the right reasons. And some of you are going to shake your

03:15:55.318 --> 03:15:58.638
heads and say, well, what are the right reasons? We're going to have that discussion.

03:16:00.963 --> 03:16:04.128
It's not the easy way out. In fact, it's the most difficult path that we

03:16:04.192 --> 03:16:06.662
have to take forward. The easy way is to say we're not going to do

03:16:07.162 --> 03:16:09.484
anything, and what we're going to do is just continue to reduce services, and we're

03:16:09.984 --> 03:16:12.146
not going to take care of those things that we, that we own and that

03:16:12.195 --> 03:16:15.643
we should be taking care of. And that's what we've done for a number of

03:16:16.143 --> 03:16:21.079
years. It's always tighten your belt, figure out a way forward without

03:16:21.176 --> 03:16:24.399
dealing with the hard problems of the fact that we

03:16:24.479 --> 03:16:28.104
don't have the resources that we need to take care of those things that we

03:16:28.604 --> 03:16:32.196
have. It really is a resourcing problem.

03:16:33.047 --> 03:16:36.644
And I can assure you this, this city council and those of

03:16:36.692 --> 03:16:40.995
the very short past have been anxiously engaged in creating economic

03:16:41.495 --> 03:16:43.821
development that is going to bear fruit.

03:16:44.479 --> 03:16:48.156
I'm confident of that. And you shake your heads no,

03:16:48.397 --> 03:16:51.978
and you can disbelieve that, but the day will come when Eagle Mountain will

03:16:52.010 --> 03:16:55.944
be known as one of the most I

03:16:56.008 --> 03:16:59.284
want to use the right word— prosperous cities

03:17:00.119 --> 03:17:03.442
in Utah County, certainly, and within the state of Utah.

03:17:04.052 --> 03:17:07.184
Those days are on our horizon, but they are not today.

03:17:07.826 --> 03:17:11.326
They are coming. And I know Mayor Gray's gotten in trouble for saying

03:17:11.342 --> 03:17:12.932
that, and I'm going to do you a favor.

03:17:14.072 --> 03:17:17.540
I'm started— I've started into my second term. This is my

03:17:18.040 --> 03:17:19.354
5th year. I'm not going to rerun.

03:17:21.153 --> 03:17:24.573
I am here to do— 30 seconds remaining. And to make the right decisions

03:17:24.605 --> 03:17:27.832
and to make the changes. And we have said no. We have

03:17:28.332 --> 03:17:32.521
said no on multiple occasions, in multiple ways, and made very hard decisions.

03:17:32.714 --> 03:17:35.925
We've adjusted the way development happens in Eagle Mountain, the way the water

03:17:36.425 --> 03:17:40.148
is allocated. We have made some tremendous strides in holding

03:17:40.180 --> 03:17:44.210
the development community accountable when they were not being held accountable prior

03:17:44.274 --> 03:17:45.173
to my arrival here.

03:17:47.330 --> 03:17:50.111
I'm sure we'll talk a little bit more about that. My time's about out,

03:17:50.225 --> 03:17:51.298
but thank you for being here.

03:17:54.195 --> 03:17:57.999
Okay, yeah, go ahead, uh, Councilor McClark. Thank you

03:17:58.499 --> 03:18:01.792
all for being here tonight. Um, I totally get

03:18:01.824 --> 03:18:04.619
why people are angry, and it makes sense that you're mistrustful,

03:18:05.533 --> 03:18:09.271
and it even makes sense why maybe you're a little bit afraid that your quality

03:18:09.771 --> 03:18:14.667
of life and your ability to take care of your family and provide for

03:18:15.167 --> 03:18:18.695
for their needs could change. And the fact is, is I feel

03:18:18.952 --> 03:18:22.401
all of those same things. Before I ran for

03:18:22.417 --> 03:18:26.541
city council, I was pissed that the city was spending money on

03:18:26.573 --> 03:18:30.391
a park that I didn't think we had money for.

03:18:30.920 --> 03:18:34.145
And I would rant and rave on Facebook, and I would tag all my elected

03:18:34.645 --> 03:18:38.059
officials and ask them to be accountable for all of that.

03:18:38.364 --> 03:18:41.579
And As I got

03:18:41.643 --> 03:18:45.311
into office, I started to realize that there's way more

03:18:46.244 --> 03:18:47.499
than I ever could have comprehended.

03:18:49.370 --> 03:18:55.565
And frankly, serving the city's an

03:18:55.742 --> 03:18:59.307
honor, but it's also super hard because

03:18:59.807 --> 03:19:03.141
there were a lot of decisions made in the early years of the city that

03:19:03.656 --> 03:19:06.946
set us up for for a trajectory that makes it

03:19:07.446 --> 03:19:08.522
so that we don't have as much control now.

03:19:11.503 --> 03:19:15.186
I want you to know too that this isn't settled. I spent a lot

03:19:15.686 --> 03:19:20.270
of time going through budgets. I spent a lot of time going through what our

03:19:22.025 --> 03:19:25.358
city's doing, what other cities are doing, and trying to

03:19:25.858 --> 03:19:29.062
find ways that we could save money so that we don't have to raise taxes.

03:19:30.476 --> 03:19:34.143
But like my colleagues up here have said, it would be easy for us

03:19:34.336 --> 03:19:37.810
just to say no and not look at the real needs that we

03:19:38.310 --> 03:19:41.676
have as a city. I think the biggest thing that we have to figure

03:19:41.692 --> 03:19:45.894
out tonight while we're meeting with you guys is what

03:19:46.119 --> 03:19:49.552
do we cut? What are the priorities that matter the

03:19:50.052 --> 03:19:53.539
most to the people of our community? Because there's

03:19:54.039 --> 03:19:57.411
more needs than we have money. Thank you. We do need to do a better

03:19:57.911 --> 03:20:00.942
job of communicating. Mayor Gray and this

03:20:01.120 --> 03:20:04.294
council have tried to come to your

03:20:04.794 --> 03:20:07.997
neighborhoods and do a listening tour. We've tried to go to

03:20:08.046 --> 03:20:11.493
farmers markets, but it's still not enough. It's still not enough,

03:20:11.720 --> 03:20:15.707
and we need to

03:20:16.207 --> 03:20:19.834
do better. I want to thank you all for being

03:20:19.866 --> 03:20:23.410
here. I hope that we get an opportunity to talk about

03:20:24.830 --> 03:20:28.338
some of the history, some of the changes, some of the reasons

03:20:28.419 --> 03:20:31.601
for the things that we do. 30 seconds remaining. Um,

03:20:31.811 --> 03:20:34.409
and I hope that you guys get a chance to stay and be a part

03:20:34.909 --> 03:20:36.636
of that conversation. So thank you.

03:20:40.073 --> 03:20:43.461
Okay, now something a little different. Uh, council kind

03:20:43.961 --> 03:20:45.844
of knew that they were going to get a chance, and as they always do,

03:20:46.644 --> 03:20:49.171
uh, but this part maybe they didn't know. I'm just going to ask, you can

03:20:49.671 --> 03:20:52.868
say no, Our City Manager Ben Reeves has

03:20:52.916 --> 03:20:56.593
been a great addition to our city and has a wealth of knowledge.

03:20:56.641 --> 03:21:00.093
I'm just going to ask if there are any comments that you'd like to

03:21:00.593 --> 03:21:03.899
make and if there's any maybe clarifying things or anything

03:21:03.931 --> 03:21:07.078
that you can help the public or the council. And you can

03:21:07.578 --> 03:21:10.562
say no. And following that, I do know that Sheriff Smith is also in the

03:21:10.578 --> 03:21:14.175
audience and give him a chance to represent the Sheriff's

03:21:14.207 --> 03:21:17.312
Department if he wishes. Uh, I don't mean to put you on the spot,

03:21:17.607 --> 03:21:19.361
but I just wanted to, to lay that out there.

03:21:22.037 --> 03:21:25.329
Thank you, Mayor. And, and I echo the comments of the council,

03:21:25.345 --> 03:21:29.186
and I echo the, the fact that it is a great honor to serve

03:21:29.218 --> 03:21:32.089
this community. Um, I've been here just a short time,

03:21:32.910 --> 03:21:36.585
and, um, but I've seen, uh, Eagle Mountain City from afar

03:21:36.713 --> 03:21:40.707
for— since its inception back in 1996 when 250

03:21:41.207 --> 03:21:44.751
residents came together to, to collectively work to build towards a brighter

03:21:45.251 --> 03:21:47.539
future. And today here we are inheriting,

03:21:48.276 --> 03:21:51.738
um, 80— a city of 80,000, and, and yet the

03:21:52.238 --> 03:21:55.872
services and the, the work and the infrastructure and everything that is

03:21:55.984 --> 03:21:59.910
needed is so essential in order to enhance and protect and

03:21:59.974 --> 03:22:02.955
preserve the quality of life that you've enjoyed for these last 30 years.

03:22:03.997 --> 03:22:08.069
Coming in new, I, I just look

03:22:08.085 --> 03:22:11.473
and applaud at our, our first responders, both with the

03:22:11.973 --> 03:22:15.391
Sheriff's Department and with, with UFA and our city staff

03:22:15.471 --> 03:22:18.875
and all that they do. And I— it's what's hard for me is that I

03:22:18.987 --> 03:22:23.419
truly feel like for many, many years that they've been working with basically

03:22:23.919 --> 03:22:26.727
an arm tied behind their back. We haven't invested in them.

03:22:26.871 --> 03:22:30.323
And, and I think there is a, a strong desire for, you know,

03:22:30.404 --> 03:22:34.466
the, the mentality of use it up, wear it out, make it do what it

03:22:34.966 --> 03:22:39.058
do without. And that's great. But there's also a certain expectation that our property

03:22:39.558 --> 03:22:42.302
values go down if we don't take care of our open spaces, if we don't

03:22:42.802 --> 03:22:45.946
take care of our parks. People go to other areas.

03:22:46.846 --> 03:22:49.800
I know it was talked a lot about the data centers. I wasn't here when

03:22:49.832 --> 03:22:53.782
the first data center was approved, but I can tell you that the incentives

03:22:53.846 --> 03:22:57.154
given to the first data center brought the next, and then that brought the next.

03:22:57.218 --> 03:23:00.751
And with each subsequent data center to today, there's been

03:23:01.251 --> 03:23:04.749
less incentive, less incentive to that. There's no incentives today, and,

03:23:04.829 --> 03:23:08.618
and they're going the other direction and bringing money to the table

03:23:09.118 --> 03:23:12.600
up front. And so if it wasn't for some of the decisions of the past,

03:23:12.777 --> 03:23:16.807
we wouldn't have inherited the bright future as well. And so it's

03:23:17.307 --> 03:23:20.870
hard to stand in judgment for those who have been here before, but I can

03:23:20.902 --> 03:23:24.116
tell you today that our city staff is about the third of the

03:23:24.616 --> 03:23:29.099
size of city staff of equivalent population,

03:23:29.697 --> 03:23:33.013
and they are working their hearts out, and they care, and they,

03:23:33.174 --> 03:23:36.786
they, they love the city that they work for, they love the council that they

03:23:37.286 --> 03:23:40.417
work for, and they love serving the people that they work for. And yet it's

03:23:40.917 --> 03:23:43.418
hard to hear after day after day that, you know,

03:23:44.032 --> 03:23:47.243
we suck, we're not keeping up, we're not keeping the parks up, we're not keeping

03:23:47.259 --> 03:23:50.269
the trails up. Well, we can only do what we can do with the staff

03:23:50.769 --> 03:23:54.990
that we have, and we can only provide that level of service that

03:23:55.022 --> 03:23:59.165
we can afford to accomplish. 30 seconds remaining. And so ultimately,

03:23:59.662 --> 03:24:02.874
this is a policy decision, and I will support the council in whatever they

03:24:03.374 --> 03:24:06.904
do, and I am absolutely confident our staff will support the council in whatever they

03:24:07.404 --> 03:24:10.677
decide, and they will do the very best with whatever resources that you entrust

03:24:11.177 --> 03:24:14.627
to them. But at the same time, please recognize

03:24:15.127 --> 03:24:18.333
that we're in it together. We are one team. We're one family. We're one Eagle

03:24:18.833 --> 03:24:21.587
Mountain City. We all live here. We all want to see our kids and grandkids

03:24:21.700 --> 03:24:24.074
live here in the future as well. Thank you.

03:24:30.093 --> 03:24:34.350
I can't see Sheriff Smith off the screen, but is he— there

03:24:34.382 --> 03:24:37.134
he is. Yep. Thank you,

03:24:37.150 --> 03:24:39.485
Mayor. Super excited to speak at something like this.

03:24:42.276 --> 03:24:45.978
So, so hey, I, you know, honestly, I get PTSD

03:24:46.140 --> 03:24:49.462
with these type of meetings. I hate them. I feel for

03:24:49.510 --> 03:24:53.067
everybody that spoke tonight. My heart goes out

03:24:53.567 --> 03:24:56.608
to you. It's a horrible thing. I feel for the council. It's a

03:24:57.108 --> 03:25:00.406
tough decision. It's easy to armchair

03:25:00.906 --> 03:25:04.372
quarterback this and criticize, but I can

03:25:04.872 --> 03:25:07.409
tell you, as somebody who sits in the seat also, it's It's a whole different

03:25:07.909 --> 03:25:10.008
story when you're sitting in the seat and you have to make the decision.

03:25:10.120 --> 03:25:13.794
So I respect that. I respect

03:25:14.294 --> 03:25:18.014
you for what you're doing. And what I will say as the sheriff is we

03:25:18.287 --> 03:25:21.913
are extremely proud to be

03:25:22.217 --> 03:25:25.667
your law enforcement out in

03:25:26.167 --> 03:25:29.068
Eagle Mountain. We are Eagle Mountain out here.

03:25:29.999 --> 03:25:33.163
The deputies that we send out here are The finest

03:25:33.663 --> 03:25:39.004
deputies that we have in the sheriff's office. You are getting high-quality

03:25:39.037 --> 03:25:42.832
people. We're able to, through the sheriff's office, we're able to recruit

03:25:44.210 --> 03:25:47.159
top officers. A lot of times, you know, I get chiefs that are mad at

03:25:47.207 --> 03:25:50.750
me. We're taking them from other cities because they want to come work for

03:25:51.250 --> 03:25:53.665
the sheriff's office. And so Eagle Mountain benefits from that.

03:25:54.696 --> 03:25:58.093
And what I will tell you is it's a

03:25:58.593 --> 03:26:03.660
tough decision to come to Eagle Mountain Council and say, hey, we need

03:26:04.160 --> 03:26:05.294
this. But as we look at crime, as we look at the growth of this

03:26:05.794 --> 03:26:09.115
city, I'll tell you, as far as the truth in

03:26:09.212 --> 03:26:11.989
taxation and what it costs to do it, again,

03:26:12.038 --> 03:26:15.409
that's a really tough decision. But that's not what I look

03:26:15.909 --> 03:26:19.359
at. I look at how do we keep Eagle Mountain safe? How do we keep

03:26:19.408 --> 03:26:22.683
Eagle Mountain a community that people want to move

03:26:22.715 --> 03:26:26.312
to, that people feel comfortable living in, that people feel like

03:26:26.812 --> 03:26:29.973
their public safety is doing their jobs. I come out and I campaign.

03:26:30.021 --> 03:26:33.361
I go to meetings and I'll tell you what I hear, not in

03:26:33.409 --> 03:26:37.279
a Truth in Taxation meeting, but what I hear overwhelmingly from people

03:26:37.889 --> 03:26:41.534
is they're upset. They're upset because they called on,

03:26:41.662 --> 03:26:45.002
you know, maybe something lower level

03:26:45.178 --> 03:26:48.855
on the food chain of law enforcement and they didn't get the response they needed.

03:26:49.995 --> 03:26:53.625
Why aren't you guys responding? And I can tell you the reason why is is

03:26:54.125 --> 03:26:57.481
because the, the deputies are basically going from call

03:26:57.981 --> 03:27:00.486
to call to call. And I heard a few comments tonight about, well, we saw

03:27:00.775 --> 03:27:04.647
them getting a drink at 7-Eleven or wherever they were. And I've

03:27:05.147 --> 03:27:08.568
told them, and I challenge them, I'm like, if you have downtime, I would rather

03:27:09.068 --> 03:27:12.794
have you— 30 seconds remaining— instead of coming to the office and hanging out,

03:27:12.923 --> 03:27:17.148
I would rather have you going somewhere like that to take a break and

03:27:17.164 --> 03:27:21.032
talk to the community, be out there with the community. So we,

03:27:21.176 --> 03:27:24.495
we want to be your police force. And what I will, what I will guarantee

03:27:24.527 --> 03:27:27.749
you as your sheriff is we're not asking for anything that's not needed out

03:27:28.249 --> 03:27:31.083
here. It is, it is definitely needed. Actually, more is needed.

03:27:31.837 --> 03:27:35.043
I've heard a few comments about doing your own police.

03:27:36.149 --> 03:27:38.730
That's on the table if they want, but I think the council knows in a

03:27:39.230 --> 03:27:42.674
feasibility study that is nowhere— it's not— Your time is up. Thank you. It's not

03:27:43.174 --> 03:27:49.304
even close to what you're getting for your Okay,

03:27:49.627 --> 03:27:53.337
thank you. And I will add a couple comments I forgot to do

03:27:53.837 --> 03:27:57.303
earlier. But some of the things like that

03:27:57.352 --> 03:28:01.222
were brought up in the meeting, impact fees and grants, those are all one-time monies.

03:28:02.970 --> 03:28:06.719
These deputies that we hire will be ongoing expenses year over year with their

03:28:07.219 --> 03:28:10.793
salaries. Just, just as thoughts for some of the questions that were

03:28:11.293 --> 03:28:14.998
brought up. Thank you. Also, I work extensively with our

03:28:15.498 --> 03:28:19.695
data center partners. I've been in office now 213 days,

03:28:20.288 --> 03:28:22.725
and I've got to know those guys very, very well.

03:28:23.558 --> 03:28:26.524
And I did— in the course of the week, I wanted to know some of

03:28:27.024 --> 03:28:30.692
the actual numbers for the community to understand. And QTS

03:28:31.192 --> 03:28:34.411
actually provided some talking points just to— You guys can take

03:28:34.911 --> 03:28:38.730
these numbers back and look at what it is that we've been telling you Coming.

03:28:39.230 --> 03:28:42.670
They're under construction. And so these, these haven't been

03:28:43.170 --> 03:28:46.320
fully realized yet. But the property tax impact from QTS,

03:28:46.670 --> 03:28:50.110
they're committed to building within our city. In the new buildings, there's 3

03:28:50.610 --> 03:28:53.290
buildings that are under construction right now. They're looking to break ground on 2 more

03:28:53.790 --> 03:28:57.530
buildings just to the north. The impact will generate over

03:28:58.030 --> 03:29:00.680
$19 million a year for our local jurisdictions.

03:29:01.830 --> 03:29:05.670
And Evan obviously knows these numbers, and we're going

03:29:06.170 --> 03:29:09.910
through these numbers every day. Yeah. Just for Eagle Mountain City alone, in our current

03:29:10.410 --> 03:29:14.030
tax rate, that Eagle Mountain City, that would be $2.7 million a

03:29:14.530 --> 03:29:17.630
year. The Utah County would get $1.7 million.

03:29:18.130 --> 03:29:21.230
Here's the big one. I heard of this a lot during this, you know,

03:29:21.730 --> 03:29:25.310
the concerns about the school district. Lake Mountain School District on the

03:29:25.810 --> 03:29:29.290
current high building would get $10.4 million a year. That's an

03:29:29.790 --> 03:29:33.390
additional $10.4 million for the budget. Every 3 years that builds an

03:29:33.890 --> 03:29:37.548
elementary school. So when I mentioned earlier on that

03:29:38.048 --> 03:29:42.014
this money is coming, these are the actual numbers. Unified Fire District

03:29:42.514 --> 03:29:45.868
under the current taxing system would get $3.2 million. We're looking by next

03:29:46.368 --> 03:29:49.717
year to bring that taxing authority back in-house. Add that to the

03:29:50.217 --> 03:29:53.922
city amount and you would see a little over $5 million to

03:29:54.422 --> 03:29:57.632
the city rather than the $2.7 million. So those are the

03:29:58.132 --> 03:30:01.830
kind of numbers Utah Central Water District would get. Okay. We get almost $1

03:30:02.330 --> 03:30:04.750
million, and then the other districts just shy of $500,000.

03:30:06.090 --> 03:30:10.210
Another thing is QTS was granted some tax

03:30:10.710 --> 03:30:14.070
incentives when they started those first 3 buildings. They came back on these next 2—

03:30:14.570 --> 03:30:17.050
we didn't even ask this— and said, hey, by the way, you guys have been

03:30:17.550 --> 03:30:20.990
so great to us, we don't want any incentives on these next builds. Just,

03:30:21.490 --> 03:30:24.950
we're going to be full taxpayers starting day one. So I want that to

03:30:25.450 --> 03:30:29.167
soak in a little bit that We are negotiating and we are

03:30:29.215 --> 03:30:32.923
working with these people, and they are going to contribute

03:30:33.423 --> 03:30:36.374
right around the corner. These dollars are going to be realized right away.

03:30:37.176 --> 03:30:40.788
We haven't even talked about the MET that that also generates. So,

03:30:41.895 --> 03:30:45.539
and another point, we were trying to figure out how to widen

03:30:45.587 --> 03:30:49.166
Pony Express here a couple of weeks ago. QTS,

03:30:50.161 --> 03:30:52.457
I made one phone call to them and said, hey, maybe we could do a

03:30:52.957 --> 03:30:56.756
reimbursement agreement, like how to work this out. That was some ideas talked about

03:30:57.256 --> 03:30:59.561
in the city hall. I called them and said, you know what, I don't feel

03:30:59.641 --> 03:31:03.184
like this is a burden the residents should bear. Some of that traffic on

03:31:03.248 --> 03:31:06.935
Pony Express is because of their workers. I said, it's about a $5 million

03:31:07.435 --> 03:31:11.552
bill with 2 stoplights, and 2 of those stoplights are areas that turn towards

03:31:11.584 --> 03:31:15.031
their development. It's about a $5 million ask,

03:31:15.095 --> 03:31:18.381
and I asked them to consider helping us

03:31:18.881 --> 03:31:22.030
with that, at least half or most. Uh, the,

03:31:22.530 --> 03:31:26.330
the people that are charged with making those decisions said,

03:31:26.830 --> 03:31:29.690
give me just a minute, hung the phone up and called me right back and

03:31:30.190 --> 03:31:33.110
said, you know what, not only will we help, we'll pay for that entire $5

03:31:33.610 --> 03:31:37.170
million bill. That road will be built. So now our internal staff,

03:31:37.670 --> 03:31:39.550
our engineers are working hard to make sure that we can get that as soon

03:31:40.050 --> 03:31:42.430
as possible. I think we'll see some of it this year and some in the

03:31:42.930 --> 03:31:45.890
future. So I just want you to know that these data centers are invested in

03:31:46.390 --> 03:31:50.169
our community. Thank you. And not just at their site, but in areas where

03:31:50.669 --> 03:31:53.405
we direct that we need some help. So, uh,

03:31:54.147 --> 03:31:57.681
some of those numbers are now out there, and you're welcome

03:31:58.181 --> 03:32:01.715
to get further information. I would say those

03:32:02.215 --> 03:32:05.668
are estimated a little bit, but we've done a lot of math over the last

03:32:06.168 --> 03:32:09.815
week just so that we can provide that with some fairly good confidence that that's

03:32:09.864 --> 03:32:12.334
the direction that we're headed on just that one site. Right.

03:32:13.515 --> 03:32:16.964
That does not include some of the other data centers. So I just wanted

03:32:17.464 --> 03:32:21.420
to get that out there as we move forward in this discussion. I think

03:32:21.920 --> 03:32:26.581
it's time— I just want to reach out to Marcus,

03:32:26.930 --> 03:32:30.818
Lacey. I know Kimberly's there. Procedurally, if we missed anything

03:32:31.498 --> 03:32:33.831
that we need to address at this time before I go on to item number

03:32:34.331 --> 03:32:34.949
4, or the council.

03:32:38.232 --> 03:32:41.173
Uh, looks like we are okay. Marcus, Kimberly, and I,

03:32:41.868 --> 03:32:44.099
we have everything we need. Thank you. I think we're good.

03:32:45.576 --> 03:32:49.514
Okay, and if the council has any questions or as we move forward,

03:32:49.708 --> 03:32:53.016
you're welcome to let me know, but I think it's time we move on

03:32:53.516 --> 03:32:56.302
to item number 4 on our agenda, which is a resolution of Eagle Mountain City

03:32:56.481 --> 03:32:59.845
approving a property tax increase above the

03:33:00.345 --> 03:33:01.844
certified tax rate for fiscal year '26-'27.

03:33:05.390 --> 03:33:08.900
I think we've talked about this a lot through our budget process and

03:33:09.400 --> 03:33:11.980
whatnot. So at this time, I'd just like to turn it over to the council

03:33:12.480 --> 03:33:14.900
if you have any questions or comments. I know there are a few of you

03:33:15.400 --> 03:33:17.640
that said that you want to get into a little more detail at this point.

03:33:18.900 --> 03:33:21.860
Uh, so I'm open to,

03:33:22.360 --> 03:33:25.940
uh, raise your hand, let me know if you'd like to speak, and we'll

03:33:26.440 --> 03:33:29.340
try to be as orderly as possible. Again, I appreciate you guys allowing me to

03:33:29.840 --> 03:33:32.024
do this remotely, but whoever wants to speak, uh, Let me know.

03:33:34.727 --> 03:33:37.964
Mayor, really quick, I think you guys have asked the question about

03:33:38.464 --> 03:33:41.862
transparency, and you're like, we want a line-by-line,

03:33:43.424 --> 03:33:46.555
uh, explanation of the budget and what the money's being

03:33:46.587 --> 03:33:50.235
spent on. And I want you guys to know that that

03:33:50.735 --> 03:33:54.651
document exists. And not only does that document exist right now for this

03:33:54.765 --> 03:33:58.535
year's budget, but it exists for all of the previous year's budgets,

03:33:58.937 --> 03:34:02.758
and they are all on our city website. There's an

03:34:02.871 --> 03:34:06.435
interactive budget so you can kind of see what the goals are

03:34:06.564 --> 03:34:10.706
for every single department, what their projects are that they're working

03:34:10.803 --> 03:34:14.640
on. So if it's parks, what parks they're upgrading.

03:34:15.138 --> 03:34:18.847
If it's the attorney's office, what contracts and different

03:34:19.347 --> 03:34:23.135
things they're working on. And it's important to us that

03:34:23.183 --> 03:34:26.991
you guys know today when you go home that that exists,

03:34:27.443 --> 03:34:30.702
and we are 100% transparent and

03:34:31.202 --> 03:34:34.834
accountable to you guys. We want you to know that it's out there. We've done

03:34:34.883 --> 03:34:37.837
a crappy job of helping you know where it is,

03:34:38.595 --> 03:34:42.956
but it exists. And I think it's really important

03:34:43.456 --> 03:34:46.080
for you guys to know that it's there. Thanks.

03:34:48.520 --> 03:34:51.620
Yeah, I think to your point, a lot of people have used the word transparency

03:34:52.120 --> 03:34:55.540
instead of communication. Transparency, everything that we've done has been in

03:34:56.040 --> 03:34:58.590
an open and public meeting, and it's very transparent.

03:34:59.600 --> 03:35:03.700
What it isn't is easy to get it out to the public in

03:35:04.200 --> 03:35:07.660
a form maybe easy to understand or whatnot. So the communication side,

03:35:08.160 --> 03:35:11.240
we've done our best, and we try, and we improve every day. But transparency is

03:35:11.740 --> 03:35:14.920
definitely— everything we've done has been in the open. So.

03:35:16.573 --> 03:35:19.683
Next, is that Council Member Huish? You go ahead.

03:35:20.424 --> 03:35:23.952
Yeah, I just want to go, go deeper into the transparency piece.

03:35:26.162 --> 03:35:30.114
When the— this is my understanding, and, and Ben, you can correct

03:35:30.614 --> 03:35:33.696
me if I misstep anywhere, okay? I'm going to be careful.

03:35:35.164 --> 03:35:38.999
But when the application was submitted for the policy to cover 100%

03:35:39.176 --> 03:35:42.580
of the sheriff's contract 100% with property taxes.

03:35:43.097 --> 03:35:46.425
That was the application. And in the state

03:35:46.925 --> 03:35:51.163
of Utah, to vary from that application would completely

03:35:51.663 --> 03:35:55.954
disrupt this entire process. So you have the communication

03:35:56.469 --> 03:36:00.295
that was put out to

03:36:00.327 --> 03:36:03.960
communicate the policy, but it's obvious that you take

03:36:04.008 --> 03:36:07.738
$6.7 million minus roughly $1.5 million

03:36:07.754 --> 03:36:10.657
and there's a gap there. It's like, well, what happens with that money,

03:36:11.396 --> 03:36:15.235
right? And that's not part of the initial application. That's a separate

03:36:15.267 --> 03:36:19.057
discussion. So I want to, if it's okay to talk

03:36:19.557 --> 03:36:23.137
about— It's okay. All right. I just want to be clear. This is something that

03:36:23.637 --> 03:36:27.521
I also struggled with in understanding and trying to communicate. So some

03:36:27.569 --> 03:36:30.396
of the things as a council that we've talked about,

03:36:31.809 --> 03:36:35.664
there are several projects going on within the city. There's also

03:36:36.164 --> 03:36:39.293
several staffing shortfalls. Number one,

03:36:39.357 --> 03:36:42.456
we've talked about getting our staff to 90%.

03:36:43.211 --> 03:36:46.053
I believe it's 90% of the median. Is that correct, Ben?

03:36:47.209 --> 03:36:50.967
We're not there. Our staff is underpaid. My neighbor used to

03:36:50.999 --> 03:36:54.773
work in the city of Eagle Mountain. He now drives to Herriman. They just pay

03:36:55.273 --> 03:36:59.413
more. He makes more money there. He is excellent with concrete

03:36:59.913 --> 03:37:03.808
work, and we train people up. We hire them here. We train them

03:37:03.938 --> 03:37:07.948
and then we lose them to other cities. And the cost of training is expensive.

03:37:08.013 --> 03:37:12.486
If for those of you that are business owners, it's expensive to train people and

03:37:12.582 --> 03:37:17.322
it's painful when you lose them because of salary.

03:37:17.821 --> 03:37:22.347
So part of the contemplation with the freed

03:37:22.847 --> 03:37:27.213
up funds is to increase based

03:37:27.278 --> 03:37:30.536
on merit and getting people up to that level to where they're

03:37:31.036 --> 03:37:33.390
not even above average, but at,

03:37:34.210 --> 03:37:37.426
at the 90% of the median, of the

03:37:37.926 --> 03:37:42.777
middle. Okay, so not even there. That cost is about $525,000

03:37:43.230 --> 03:37:46.872
to do that. So there's that. There's also

03:37:47.372 --> 03:37:51.225
the contemplation. These, these are positions that would impact

03:37:51.937 --> 03:37:55.399
the general fund. There's about 18

03:37:55.997 --> 03:37:59.597
part-time and full-time positions. 13 of those,

03:38:00.673 --> 03:38:04.444
in my estimation, have to do with our parks. And I'm just gonna— I won't

03:38:04.944 --> 03:38:08.424
go through each specific salary, but I would like to mention,

03:38:09.242 --> 03:38:12.997
uh, the, the different jobs. So we, we've contemplated,

03:38:13.735 --> 03:38:16.592
uh, hiring 3 open space,

03:38:17.667 --> 03:38:20.861
uh, employees for— So when

03:38:20.893 --> 03:38:24.551
you're on Eagle Mountain Citizens page and you see someone complaining about My trail needs

03:38:24.583 --> 03:38:27.196
to be cleaned. We've got trails all over. We've got,

03:38:27.869 --> 03:38:31.060
I don't know, hundreds of miles of trail. I have them behind my

03:38:31.076 --> 03:38:34.843
house and they get overgrown. And we've, we've got a staff where

03:38:35.343 --> 03:38:38.658
that's what they're out doing. We've contemplated having

03:38:38.771 --> 03:38:42.137
3 parks technicians. The other

03:38:42.637 --> 03:38:45.183
day I was at Quarry Ride, and I'm sorry if I'm going— is it okay?

03:38:45.488 --> 03:38:48.758
Go. Okay. I just have a lot to say. I've been thinking

03:38:48.790 --> 03:38:52.946
about this a lot. I was at Corey Ride at the dunk tank

03:38:53.446 --> 03:38:56.653
actually yesterday with the Youth Council, and I was walking around the park and I

03:38:57.153 --> 03:39:00.504
was like, the grass actually looks good. The last time I was Corey

03:39:01.004 --> 03:39:04.901
Ride, it looked terrible. And I thought, we sold our utility for this park and

03:39:05.401 --> 03:39:08.929
we just let it go. And I happened to run into one of the parks

03:39:09.073 --> 03:39:12.459
techs that was there. I said, you've got—

03:39:12.959 --> 03:39:15.572
this park looks good. I don't know what you're doing, but it does not look

03:39:16.072 --> 03:39:18.864
like the park by my house. The park by my house is dying. There's weeds

03:39:19.364 --> 03:39:22.896
everywhere, and you can drive around Eagle Mountain and see

03:39:23.396 --> 03:39:27.072
those conditions. And he said, you know, we took this park back in-house,

03:39:27.297 --> 03:39:30.943
meaning our employees are taking care of this park. We have a lot

03:39:31.443 --> 03:39:34.702
of contract work that happens throughout the city in our parks,

03:39:34.782 --> 03:39:38.765
and they don't look great. So we've contemplated hiring

03:39:39.263 --> 03:39:42.170
more parks crews so that we can actually control the quality and service level.

03:39:42.251 --> 03:39:45.630
Thank you. We've considered

03:39:45.760 --> 03:39:46.573
recreation.

03:39:49.492 --> 03:39:53.577
We have brought soccer back in-house. So our

03:39:54.077 --> 03:39:56.858
ball fields, our soccer fields are not in great condition.

03:39:57.940 --> 03:40:01.515
Some of the kids that are playing are skinning their knees. Maybe that's

03:40:02.015 --> 03:40:06.179
okay. For me, I think we can do better there. We've also contemplated

03:40:06.679 --> 03:40:10.804
a streets operator, sealing the cracks. Now it's true when you build a development

03:40:10.998 --> 03:40:14.313
and you charge impact fees to a developer, that developer is

03:40:14.813 --> 03:40:18.572
not on the hook for the maintenance in perpetuity. There's just not a fund that

03:40:19.072 --> 03:40:22.766
exists to maintain those streets, and there are

03:40:23.266 --> 03:40:27.176
real costs. So we've contemplated hiring there. There's also 5 positions

03:40:27.208 --> 03:40:30.712
considered around engineering

03:40:30.970 --> 03:40:35.350
and just day-to-day operations. We have a lot of Well,

03:40:35.815 --> 03:40:39.712
Ben can probably speak to this, to turnover within the city regarding

03:40:39.808 --> 03:40:44.090
HR. Our purchasing agent is overwhelmed.

03:40:44.218 --> 03:40:47.410
Last year asked for— to hire somebody.

03:40:48.164 --> 03:40:51.179
We did not hire that person. She's working a lot of overtime,

03:40:51.756 --> 03:40:56.230
and, you know, it's emotionally exhausting. So these

03:40:56.262 --> 03:40:59.358
are some of the things that freed up funds

03:40:59.454 --> 03:41:02.825
that we're contemplating. Thank you. This was

03:41:03.082 --> 03:41:06.133
in the May 26th meeting. I talked about this specifically.

03:41:07.160 --> 03:41:10.163
And you can go listen to those. I know nobody wants to go listen to

03:41:10.211 --> 03:41:13.358
6 hours of budget meetings of city council. That's not your job. That's our job.

03:41:13.743 --> 03:41:17.340
But these are things that weren't communicated, I think out of a fear of

03:41:17.597 --> 03:41:20.519
crossing the line with the application that was filled.

03:41:20.584 --> 03:41:24.533
So I just want to clarify that. And if there's anything that

03:41:25.033 --> 03:41:28.089
I communicated in in the podcast that I put out there was not intended to

03:41:28.589 --> 03:41:31.730
mislead anybody or to confuse anyone. It was meant to bring

03:41:31.826 --> 03:41:35.306
more context that I didn't feel like was being put out there in, um,

03:41:35.628 --> 03:41:39.027
in the Eagle Mountain Citizens page. So I'll just pause there on transparency,

03:41:39.075 --> 03:41:41.878
and I have more around policy that I'd love to discuss.

03:41:43.639 --> 03:41:47.141
Yeah, and I think the, the key to that, Zach, what you're talking about

03:41:47.641 --> 03:41:51.242
is, uh, more about the budget and the budget process than,

03:41:51.356 --> 03:41:54.411
than so much this tax, but just all of the different things in our budget

03:41:54.911 --> 03:41:58.267
that we are dealing with, right? So appreciate the context.

03:41:59.231 --> 03:42:02.667
Um, I think Councilmember Wood, you had some other comments,

03:42:02.715 --> 03:42:05.541
or Councilmember Whiting, go ahead. Yep, thank you, Mayor.

03:42:05.670 --> 03:42:09.074
So we were asked earlier to answer the question,

03:42:09.122 --> 03:42:12.783
what are the, you know, 3 capital projects you would spend the money

03:42:12.799 --> 03:42:16.395
on? And I'm going to jump on what Councilmember Huish already

03:42:16.588 --> 03:42:20.329
indicated, that our parks and open space, it's very apparent. You just drive around and

03:42:20.361 --> 03:42:23.491
you see their condition. So, so before

03:42:23.991 --> 03:42:27.391
I answer the question, that, that question, let me back up and support something.

03:42:28.081 --> 03:42:31.275
So when we, when we say that 100% of the public,

03:42:31.355 --> 03:42:34.806
of the, the property tax increase goes to public safety, that is the

03:42:35.306 --> 03:42:39.059
correct answer. What we need to do is, is help you

03:42:39.559 --> 03:42:42.381
understand, and this, this means we didn't communicate this well enough,

03:42:43.697 --> 03:42:48.463
how public safety has been paid. So right now it's being paid for

03:42:48.963 --> 03:42:52.499
through property tax, and we're essentially robbing from sales

03:42:52.531 --> 03:42:56.216
tax to help subsidize our

03:42:56.716 --> 03:43:01.232
police and our public safety. So this communication

03:43:01.344 --> 03:43:04.918
that we're— that 100% of the tax increase goes to public safety is

03:43:05.014 --> 03:43:08.315
true. Now puts property tax as the pillar upon which public safety

03:43:08.815 --> 03:43:12.289
now stands. And this is what we need to communicate better. What that now does

03:43:12.321 --> 03:43:15.915
is is it, it, it takes the,

03:43:16.076 --> 03:43:19.221
the sales tax dollars that we were essentially robbing from sales tax to

03:43:19.721 --> 03:43:22.351
go support public safety, and now it takes that sales tax dollars to do other

03:43:22.851 --> 03:43:27.053
things. And that's what Councilmember Herwish was referring to, the several different hires

03:43:27.085 --> 03:43:29.701
within the city, wage increases for the employees.

03:43:30.439 --> 03:43:33.777
And, and so that's— and perhaps that's what some of you are wondering,

03:43:34.371 --> 03:43:38.046
like, okay, what is now, uh, what can now be paid

03:43:38.062 --> 03:43:42.059
for? Property tax is now taken public safety, what can we now do with sales

03:43:42.091 --> 03:43:45.126
tax dollars that were being used toward public safety, what can we now do

03:43:45.626 --> 03:43:48.112
with them? We're stating some of it. If you go back and look at our

03:43:48.612 --> 03:43:52.607
budgets, we said in the budget what we would do with that money, including several

03:43:53.107 --> 03:43:56.348
understaffed departments. Our city

03:43:56.848 --> 03:44:00.522
manager commented we're about a third the staffing level as

03:44:01.341 --> 03:44:04.536
other cities of our size. With that being said,

03:44:04.970 --> 03:44:08.631
these dollars that are available now through sales tax. My number one

03:44:08.808 --> 03:44:11.474
thing is public safety on our streets.

03:44:12.679 --> 03:44:16.052
We have a massive need right now for safer streets,

03:44:16.389 --> 03:44:20.790
especially in our school zones. And we need, we need speed tables

03:44:21.290 --> 03:44:24.644
and other mechanisms to keep the kids safe. It may

03:44:25.144 --> 03:44:28.402
shock you, but a speed table costs about $70,000 to $80,000.

03:44:29.141 --> 03:44:32.410
And we don't just need one. We don't just need two. We need

03:44:32.508 --> 03:44:35.263
a ton of them. That costs a lot of money.

03:44:36.916 --> 03:44:40.869
If this tax— this doesn't go through, what now happens? Well, the sales

03:44:41.369 --> 03:44:45.209
tax will have to go back to support, you know, our public safety,

03:44:45.790 --> 03:44:49.265
and it gets diverted from being able to put speed tables and other things like

03:44:49.765 --> 03:44:53.324
that in our school zones and other streets. There was comment

03:44:53.824 --> 03:44:56.804
that I know, you know, there's some of you that I know well that have

03:44:56.905 --> 03:45:00.289
come up and made public comment. I know that you live on streets

03:45:00.322 --> 03:45:03.568
that are not safe. Your streets are being studied right

03:45:03.600 --> 03:45:08.511
now. We commissioned a study that is being done on

03:45:09.011 --> 03:45:12.686
sensitive streets in the city, and so that we can get the best data

03:45:12.702 --> 03:45:16.881
available on how to bring those cities to

03:45:16.898 --> 03:45:20.737
a safer level, so we can slow down traffic,

03:45:21.300 --> 03:45:24.876
so kids can cross safely. And that again goes

03:45:24.908 --> 03:45:29.131
back to transparency. I think that's a message we didn't clearly communicate

03:45:29.307 --> 03:45:32.567
well enough to the city, because had that been part of the

03:45:33.067 --> 03:45:36.549
discussion, I think people would be like, oh, wait a second, I can get

03:45:37.049 --> 03:45:40.242
behind freeing up some sales tax dollars to

03:45:40.742 --> 03:45:44.015
go toward making sure our streets are safer. We just didn't

03:45:44.515 --> 03:45:47.532
communicate that, which is why I'm happy you're here. This is part of the discussion

03:45:48.032 --> 03:45:52.153
we can now have. to help educate and

03:45:52.282 --> 03:45:55.350
get the information out there as to what we're doing and why

03:45:55.850 --> 03:45:58.262
this increase is so important. I'll leave it at that for the time being.

03:45:58.327 --> 03:46:01.829
I've got comments on— well, maybe let me just say this so I

03:46:02.329 --> 03:46:04.847
can wrap up. I just had one additional comment on the data center thing.

03:46:05.500 --> 03:46:09.010
We're hearing people saying, we're not seeing anything from the data centers. I'm gonna tell

03:46:09.510 --> 03:46:13.160
you something. You are wrong, and I'll tell you why. Everyone,

03:46:14.202 --> 03:46:18.748
in this city who is 100%— 120%

03:46:19.327 --> 03:46:22.845
area median income or lower can qualify for

03:46:22.957 --> 03:46:24.372
a down payment to buy a house.

03:46:26.803 --> 03:46:30.377
That money is coming from data centers so

03:46:30.877 --> 03:46:34.403
that you can— that down payment money just doesn't magically appear. It is Facebook,

03:46:35.192 --> 03:46:39.250
and I believe Tyson, that's putting money into an

03:46:39.299 --> 03:46:42.852
RDA from which we are— we have created the most successful

03:46:43.352 --> 03:46:45.950
down payment mortgage down payment assistance program in the state.

03:46:46.854 --> 03:46:50.934
It gives you more money and is more easily qualified for than any

03:46:51.434 --> 03:46:54.708
other city. And maybe that again comes down to communication.

03:46:56.220 --> 03:46:59.595
That is available. There's millions of dollars that's already been given

03:47:00.439 --> 03:47:03.642
to citizens of Eagle Mountain to buy homes. Down payment is not

03:47:04.142 --> 03:47:07.615
something that anybody needs to buy a home in this city. You don't need that.

03:47:07.663 --> 03:47:11.662
If you're saying, I don't have a down payment, the down payment is available through

03:47:11.984 --> 03:47:15.469
the RDAs that was set up that our data centers are funding

03:47:15.517 --> 03:47:18.055
into. And it's only going to get bigger.

03:47:19.292 --> 03:47:22.745
We just approved— and this is another conversation, and I'll probably talk about this later

03:47:23.245 --> 03:47:26.343
in the meeting— part of the reason why we arrived to

03:47:26.843 --> 03:47:30.439
the, to the area, to this situation, circumstance that we have arrived to, is we

03:47:30.939 --> 03:47:33.684
have built the city upon residential housing.

03:47:35.082 --> 03:47:38.334
And the challenge that owner-occupied residential housing, which I support,

03:47:39.150 --> 03:47:42.320
but owner-occupied housing gets a 45% decrease on their

03:47:42.336 --> 03:47:47.673
taxes. And so the

03:47:48.173 --> 03:47:50.731
growth issue is one reason why I ran. I've been on the city council for

03:47:51.231 --> 03:47:54.572
a few months now, by the way. And so that— not that it's

03:47:55.072 --> 03:47:57.449
relevant or irrelevant, but I've been on here just a few months. Thank you.

03:47:57.672 --> 03:48:01.297
I got involved because of growth that was unsustainable.

03:48:02.083 --> 03:48:05.853
And so what happens is when prior city councils— I'm not going

03:48:06.353 --> 03:48:10.490
to accuse them of their decisions, but they made decisions where they granted

03:48:10.990 --> 03:48:14.966
developers the ability to build thousands of homes

03:48:16.024 --> 03:48:20.581
at the fastest pace they want to build. There's 25,000

03:48:20.773 --> 03:48:23.789
vested units that still exist that we can't get rid of.

03:48:24.564 --> 03:48:27.383
And those can be built whenever those developers want to build it.

03:48:27.785 --> 03:48:31.618
And so once you give the blank check to the developers

03:48:31.651 --> 03:48:34.562
to be able to build as fast as they want to build, how can we

03:48:35.062 --> 03:48:38.207
then manage the growth? Because they can build as fast as they want.

03:48:39.021 --> 03:48:42.753
And when they build, yeah, we're collecting impact fees. But— and that came

03:48:43.253 --> 03:48:46.690
up a lot too tonight— impact fees, we are charging them.

03:48:46.931 --> 03:48:50.442
And guess what? We're charging as much as we're allowed to charge. I would love

03:48:50.554 --> 03:48:53.970
for impact fees to pay for all of this stuff. The state of Utah has

03:48:54.082 --> 03:48:57.578
laws about how impact fees can be charged. We're charging the maximum we can charge.

03:48:57.867 --> 03:49:01.668
I'm just letting you all know that. But, but those impact fees, the state has

03:49:01.764 --> 03:49:05.805
come in for whatever reasons it's come in and curbed how

03:49:06.305 --> 03:49:09.301
much we can charge. So we charge as much as we can. But is it

03:49:09.801 --> 03:49:12.797
enough? It's not enough. But we are, we are charging as much as we can.

03:49:12.845 --> 03:49:16.120
So when citizens come up and say, what about impact fees? It's true that

03:49:16.620 --> 03:49:20.467
we should ask that question. We're charging what we can charge, but the impact

03:49:20.967 --> 03:49:24.058
is clearly not being paid for correctly, right? I mean,

03:49:24.090 --> 03:49:26.322
there's— they're creating more impact than we can collect.

03:49:27.818 --> 03:49:31.210
But anyway, I bring this up because you

03:49:31.710 --> 03:49:35.394
need to know that this city council this year— I want

03:49:35.491 --> 03:49:38.943
you to guess how many new housing units has

03:49:39.121 --> 03:49:42.782
this city council approved? Zero. We have approved

03:49:43.586 --> 03:49:45.676
33 total.

03:49:47.444 --> 03:49:51.472
Is growth being managed? It's being managed. How many

03:49:51.892 --> 03:49:55.061
developers have— just Twasik alone, I had 2 developers

03:49:55.561 --> 03:49:57.988
reach out to me privately, not to these— to my fellow city council members.

03:49:58.537 --> 03:50:01.189
Craig, I want to do this high-density development here. I want to do this development

03:50:01.689 --> 03:50:04.616
here. And I said, it's not the time and it's not going to happen.

03:50:04.907 --> 03:50:07.817
We're not going to support it. It's not going to happen. And they just— and

03:50:07.833 --> 03:50:10.987
they left. You don't see the gates that we are

03:50:11.036 --> 03:50:14.738
defending. We are defending this castle right now in a way that I can't

03:50:15.238 --> 03:50:19.011
say hasn't ever been defended before. But since I've been involved here and the city

03:50:19.043 --> 03:50:22.587
council has been unified in this, we are managing residential growth

03:50:23.513 --> 03:50:27.037
exceptionally well because it's these— it's the

03:50:27.537 --> 03:50:31.914
additional residents that are coming in paying 2010 taxes that

03:50:32.107 --> 03:50:35.622
we cannot sustain. That math does not add up when we're

03:50:35.686 --> 03:50:39.479
charging those new residents, those new households, 2010 taxes And

03:50:39.979 --> 03:50:43.581
we're trying to support them through 2026 expenses. Every new resident

03:50:44.081 --> 03:50:46.381
that comes in, we cannot sustain it.

03:50:48.370 --> 03:50:52.043
There are not— there's not enough being collected from the new households. The growth— you're

03:50:52.543 --> 03:50:55.215
saying, well, we have a larger tax base. Revenues have increased because we have a

03:50:55.715 --> 03:50:58.176
larger tax base, but expenses have increased much faster.

03:50:59.243 --> 03:51:01.523
So when people say the growth should pay for it, the growth is the issue

03:51:02.023 --> 03:51:04.805
because we are growing under a 2010 tax rate.

03:51:05.698 --> 03:51:08.765
Even the data centers say, well, get the data centers to pay more money.

03:51:08.845 --> 03:51:12.121
I would love if we could tax the data centers separately. The state of

03:51:12.137 --> 03:51:15.509
Utah doesn't allow it. I would love to do it. We cannot

03:51:16.009 --> 03:51:19.523
do that. It's illegal to do that. So guess what? The data centers,

03:51:19.539 --> 03:51:22.461
if they had sent a representative here, would have also been standing at the very

03:51:22.493 --> 03:51:25.994
pulpit you stood at and would have said, we oppose the tax increase.

03:51:26.893 --> 03:51:30.281
They would have all done that. They are laughing

03:51:31.469 --> 03:51:34.519
all the way to the bank right now that they are only paying 2010

03:51:35.019 --> 03:51:38.580
taxes, and the only way we can make them pay more is to

03:51:38.628 --> 03:51:42.609
increase the tax rate. And that tax rate has to be increased on

03:51:43.283 --> 03:51:46.943
that same tax rate that we, that we could vote on today. That applies

03:51:47.281 --> 03:51:50.587
ubiquitously across the city. That's the only way to

03:51:51.087 --> 03:51:53.493
get them to pay more. I wish we could get them to pay more otherwise.

03:51:53.557 --> 03:51:57.008
We just cannot. And so it's, it's important for

03:51:57.508 --> 03:51:59.988
us to know that growth, unsustainable.

03:52:01.164 --> 03:52:04.647
That has caused us to be in the situation we're at today where

03:52:05.147 --> 03:52:08.435
we suddenly have to make a decision that's difficult. Anyway, I will— I'll pass

03:52:08.553 --> 03:52:09.125
the microphone down.

03:52:13.900 --> 03:52:17.552
Mayor, are you okay if I talk? Yeah, go ahead. I just want to remind

03:52:18.052 --> 03:52:22.013
the council too on item number 4, I mean, free to speak. I'm not limiting

03:52:22.029 --> 03:52:25.974
debate necessarily. I'd just like to keep it to maybe

03:52:26.474 --> 03:52:30.021
your position on the tax increase and supporting your position.

03:52:30.310 --> 03:52:33.087
And I understand everything we've heard is in that manner,

03:52:33.136 --> 03:52:36.780
but just to— With

03:52:36.813 --> 03:52:39.767
all due respect, Mayor, I think that both of them have kept on topic with

03:52:39.783 --> 03:52:43.299
why people are looking for answers as to what is motivating these

03:52:43.717 --> 03:52:47.185
decisions. And I think we need to probably err on the side of

03:52:47.685 --> 03:52:50.613
allowing the council to give an explanation. explanation of why we're going to tax people.

03:52:51.677 --> 03:52:55.516
So that's exactly what I'm asking. Okay, what I said— I,

03:52:58.218 --> 03:53:01.072
first of all, thank you to both of the council members who have expressed a

03:53:01.120 --> 03:53:04.216
lot of the things that I think have been part

03:53:04.716 --> 03:53:06.959
of the decision-making process for me. Um,

03:53:08.621 --> 03:53:11.995
all of them will corroborate what I'm about to say. When we had this discussion,

03:53:12.415 --> 03:53:16.072
uh, and, uh, I was actually remote It was a discussion that

03:53:16.572 --> 03:53:20.412
began and probably ended with our

03:53:20.912 --> 03:53:24.139
retreat in St. George. But one of the things that I said, and I still

03:53:24.639 --> 03:53:28.183
stand by this, the only thing anybody's ever going to remember is 220%.

03:53:28.248 --> 03:53:31.857
Doesn't matter what else we say.

03:53:32.276 --> 03:53:35.736
The only thing they're going to hear is 220%. So we have to

03:53:36.236 --> 03:53:39.741
be concise and clear about how we

03:53:40.241 --> 03:53:43.073
express to them why that even makes any sense to anybody.

03:53:45.290 --> 03:53:48.630
The reason that I voted for us to put that certified tax

03:53:49.130 --> 03:53:52.713
rate at the level that it was is because I believe a fiscal responsibility

03:53:53.294 --> 03:53:56.953
to fund our police force, our public safety, with a

03:53:57.018 --> 03:54:00.335
stable revenue. That is not sales tax.

03:54:01.402 --> 03:54:05.189
And I, I appreciate the head shakes. I do. Because let me explain that

03:54:05.689 --> 03:54:08.173
to you, because I want you to understand where I'm coming from.

03:54:09.900 --> 03:54:13.966
What happened when the pandemic happened and sales tax wasn't providing

03:54:13.998 --> 03:54:16.373
anything for anybody? Did we just stop having police force?

03:54:18.519 --> 03:54:21.745
What happened when the market crashed and

03:54:21.907 --> 03:54:25.343
everybody started losing their houses? Did we suddenly not have a need for police force?

03:54:25.408 --> 03:54:29.284
In fact, we had a greater need for police force. There isn't a single

03:54:29.784 --> 03:54:33.252
person who lives in this city that does not pay a property tax of some

03:54:33.348 --> 03:54:37.414
sort that goes directly to the police force

03:54:37.640 --> 03:54:41.270
under this particular proposal. Perhaps the

03:54:41.770 --> 03:54:46.349
rate's wrong, but I think in principle, a stable revenue

03:54:46.849 --> 03:54:49.298
source to pay for public safety should be a principle that we stand on.

03:54:50.136 --> 03:54:53.774
We have been woefully arrogant,

03:54:54.810 --> 03:54:55.764
and we have been,

03:54:58.588 --> 03:55:01.795
I'm not going to be loose with our lack

03:55:02.295 --> 03:55:05.595
of financial plans in this city. We haven't had one.

03:55:06.910 --> 03:55:10.085
When I got here, I said, where's our 5-year plan? Where's our 10-year plan?

03:55:10.775 --> 03:55:14.287
Do we have a 20-year plan? The answer is no to all

03:55:14.787 --> 03:55:18.200
of those things. So when you're asking how come you can't

03:55:18.700 --> 03:55:22.561
provide that information, I'm asking the same thing. Where is the information

03:55:23.061 --> 03:55:25.592
that we're making these decisions on, and how are we preparing for the future.

03:55:26.377 --> 03:55:30.768
I, I think it's interesting when you have past council members come

03:55:30.816 --> 03:55:34.229
in and say things like, uh, how, how dare you did that, we were able

03:55:34.245 --> 03:55:37.385
to live on the income that we had there. And I think we're here

03:55:37.529 --> 03:55:40.574
because of your irresponsibility. You left this

03:55:41.074 --> 03:55:45.044
big pile for us. You didn't raise fees for

03:55:45.544 --> 03:55:48.874
water, you didn't raise fees for sewer, almost to the point where we're in

03:55:48.890 --> 03:55:52.255
the red. You didn't raise property taxes because of

03:55:52.755 --> 03:55:57.323
some crazy conservative fiscal federal mentality

03:55:57.501 --> 03:56:00.796
of I'm not going to raise taxes because somehow money appears

03:56:00.893 --> 03:56:04.629
out of nowhere. And it left us in a position where we

03:56:05.129 --> 03:56:08.671
hear you when you say our parks are in poor repair,

03:56:09.302 --> 03:56:12.760
our parkways are not being taken care of, I can't ride my motorcycle

03:56:12.776 --> 03:56:15.038
down the road because all we can afford to do is crack seal.

03:56:17.315 --> 03:56:20.658
We can't raise the level of How we're taking care

03:56:21.158 --> 03:56:24.080
of the city because we don't have the funds to do so because people in

03:56:24.096 --> 03:56:28.627
the past thought that being cheap was a good way of public

03:56:28.675 --> 03:56:32.258
policy. So I find myself in this situation now

03:56:32.531 --> 03:56:34.588
of how do we course correct this?

03:56:35.503 --> 03:56:37.897
Some of you have said, how can we do it incrementally?

03:56:38.990 --> 03:56:41.545
If you want to continue to kick the can down the road, we can do

03:56:42.045 --> 03:56:45.304
that. You will find that we cannot keep on pace, exactly what Craig was

03:56:45.804 --> 03:56:49.037
saying. Thank you. We are adding 1,800 building permits a year.

03:56:50.637 --> 03:56:53.855
That is not sustainable for our roads, for our public safety,

03:56:54.964 --> 03:56:58.711
for fire services. We're 50 square miles. There is

03:56:58.793 --> 03:57:02.327
no other city that is experiencing the problems that we have with

03:57:02.664 --> 03:57:06.568
basically 2 separate cities that existed forever with

03:57:06.585 --> 03:57:09.654
nothing in between, and we had to service both with water,

03:57:09.670 --> 03:57:12.847
sewer, public safety, fire services.

03:57:14.875 --> 03:57:18.255
So we're left in this place with, well, what's the decision to be made

03:57:18.755 --> 03:57:22.010
then? Everybody's going to hate the decision. If I came in and said in the

03:57:22.510 --> 03:57:25.572
very beginning we're going to raise the taxes by 5%, would anybody raise their hand

03:57:26.072 --> 03:57:29.333
and say, yeah, I'm in favor of you raising taxes? The answer

03:57:29.833 --> 03:57:33.016
is going to be no. We're raising taxes. I'm against it. I don't

03:57:33.032 --> 03:57:36.281
want to pay it. It affects me. We are a one-income family as well,

03:57:36.329 --> 03:57:39.383
by the way. And it affects our ability to be able to make our bills

03:57:39.883 --> 03:57:44.114
as well. I also pushed very, very hard to have a corridor

03:57:44.322 --> 03:57:47.706
that goes behind my house that ruins the sight

03:57:48.011 --> 03:57:51.347
and sound of my area I live in in Silver Lake that's quiet,

03:57:51.860 --> 03:57:54.907
and it will not be that anymore. I also voted to make sure that we

03:57:54.923 --> 03:57:58.259
built a road from Brookhaven up to the upper part of

03:57:58.451 --> 03:58:02.172
Silver Lake out to Pony Express so there was a safe walking route for

03:58:02.672 --> 03:58:05.739
the kids. And guess whose house that goes by? Mine.

03:58:06.807 --> 03:58:09.975
I took all the traffic. It's not good for me. It's bad

03:58:10.475 --> 03:58:13.978
for my family. My kids can't go out on the street, but it created an

03:58:14.478 --> 03:58:15.855
opportunity for them to walk to school.

03:58:17.701 --> 03:58:20.874
Craig brought up the speed tables. I think you need to know how

03:58:21.374 --> 03:58:24.825
passionate we are about making sure that number one, every one of the elementary

03:58:25.325 --> 03:58:29.021
schools has sufficient safe ways to stop traffic

03:58:29.038 --> 03:58:32.146
so that they can cross. The crossing guards shouldn't have to feel like

03:58:32.646 --> 03:58:35.480
they're going to get hit. And then we have a speed study that is going

03:58:35.496 --> 03:58:38.653
to address every other road that has the same problem. We had a community come

03:58:38.717 --> 03:58:42.226
in, uh, 2, 2 meetings ago talking about

03:58:42.726 --> 03:58:46.024
the speed in their community, and they've been told multiple times, you know, we'll find

03:58:46.524 --> 03:58:50.383
a solution, then nothing's happened. The one thing I want you to walk away from

03:58:50.399 --> 03:58:54.005
this today, regardless of what our decision is, there isn't anybody

03:58:54.505 --> 03:58:58.114
up here that is looking for an easy way out. I hope that

03:58:58.614 --> 03:59:01.133
there isn't anybody that's trying to make the decisions for a political reason,

03:59:01.807 --> 03:59:05.853
because I think that's a coward's way out. We should be doing what's right regardless

03:59:05.918 --> 03:59:09.755
of the, of the outcome. And for me, that's how

03:59:10.255 --> 03:59:14.331
I will vote. I'll vote for what is best for 80,000 people. I'm not

03:59:14.831 --> 03:59:15.584
going to do what's best for a few.

03:59:20.513 --> 03:59:23.367
Mayor, is it okay if I go? Yes, sir, please.

03:59:24.746 --> 03:59:27.938
I would just echo everything that's been said. I think something that hasn't been hit

03:59:27.954 --> 03:59:31.546
on is transportation. The biggest issue that we face in Eagle Mountain

03:59:31.642 --> 03:59:35.427
is transportation and our transportation corridors. We sit in traffic

03:59:35.927 --> 03:59:39.324
far too often and far too long. We have intersections that are missing,

03:59:40.222 --> 03:59:43.494
uh, traffic control signals because we can't afford to put them in.

03:59:44.793 --> 03:59:48.870
Where does that money come from? If we continue to pull sales

03:59:48.902 --> 03:59:52.675
tax dollars, which could fund those, those infrastructure improvements, and we

03:59:53.175 --> 03:59:56.817
continue to allocate it to our sheriff's contract or to our public services.

03:59:58.229 --> 04:00:01.874
Those are the things that we're really facing. Every time that

04:00:01.906 --> 04:00:04.619
we decide we need a road to be a little bit bigger, we've got to

04:00:04.699 --> 04:00:07.910
upsize some sewer, we've got to do some sort of improvement,

04:00:07.926 --> 04:00:11.442
that comes out of the taxpayers' dollars. It comes

04:00:11.942 --> 04:00:14.765
out of the general fund, and we have to decide, are we going to set

04:00:15.265 --> 04:00:18.203
some money aside take care of those improvements, or are we going to continue to

04:00:18.703 --> 04:00:21.979
fund as it grows? Because I'm going to tell you this, should we

04:00:22.479 --> 04:00:24.985
choose to approve this at 220% this year,

04:00:26.086 --> 04:00:29.290
next year that is not going to cover 100% of the sheriff's contract.

04:00:29.888 --> 04:00:32.963
News flash. Because their fuel is going to go up,

04:00:33.351 --> 04:00:36.383
they're going to get raises, and they're going to be back in here next year

04:00:36.415 --> 04:00:39.575
saying, you guys added another 6,000 people, we need

04:00:40.075 --> 04:00:43.255
a couple more officers. So it's not something that's going away.

04:00:44.205 --> 04:00:48.102
It's just something that we are being asked to— what do we do to adjust

04:00:48.150 --> 04:00:52.183
this policy so that we can get to a better place today and

04:00:52.280 --> 04:00:53.914
work for a better future tomorrow?

04:00:56.796 --> 04:01:00.335
We've had a lot of hard conversations one-on-one. We've had a

04:01:00.351 --> 04:01:03.649
lot of dialogue, as we said in that retreat. We've had a lot of

04:01:03.713 --> 04:01:06.993
dialogue as we've had those opportunities in public meetings

04:01:07.026 --> 04:01:08.894
to have the discussion during the budget sessions.

04:01:13.903 --> 04:01:17.439
And I'm just going to say this. I'm proud to serve with the council

04:01:17.939 --> 04:01:21.138
members who are to my left and to my right. They are not cowards.

04:01:25.799 --> 04:01:28.948
It's not easy to face you, and it's not easy to tell you the truth,

04:01:30.851 --> 04:01:31.980
but that's what we've chosen to do.

04:01:39.012 --> 04:01:42.105
Yeah, I want to at least answer Melanie's question.

04:01:43.483 --> 04:01:46.736
Everything that's been said up here is exactly how I think all of us feel.

04:01:47.826 --> 04:01:51.672
And we hope that it's kind of a glimpse into some

04:01:52.172 --> 04:01:55.887
of the thought process that we've put into this, the things

04:01:55.919 --> 04:01:59.061
that we've wrestled with, the questions that we've been asking ourselves.

04:01:59.695 --> 04:02:03.348
But Melanie asked, like, what are the capital projects,

04:02:03.365 --> 04:02:05.427
top 3? And for me,

04:02:07.166 --> 04:02:10.464
obviously roads, safety, getting kids

04:02:10.836 --> 04:02:14.102
to school safely. Those are things where there's no special

04:02:14.135 --> 04:02:18.224
fees coming from someplace else. There's no additional dollars coming

04:02:18.256 --> 04:02:21.486
from anywhere. And we have a

04:02:21.502 --> 04:02:25.378
family of 7 in our house. Wow. and I was

04:02:25.878 --> 04:02:29.631
a teacher for 10 years, so it's important for me for both kids and

04:02:30.131 --> 04:02:31.846
adults to be able to get where they need to go safely.

04:02:33.162 --> 04:02:36.741
Every minute that you spend in your car is time that you're not

04:02:37.158 --> 04:02:40.625
with your family. It's time that you can't be at your

04:02:41.125 --> 04:02:44.557
kids' games or spending time with your wife or

04:02:45.057 --> 04:02:48.280
husband or your aged parents. So, like, roads are

04:02:48.553 --> 04:02:52.355
crazy important to me, and they will always be crazy important to me. But the

04:02:52.855 --> 04:02:55.837
next 2 things for me are ones that aren't sexy,

04:02:56.591 --> 04:03:00.875
and they're not ones that a lot of people think about. But I

04:03:00.907 --> 04:03:04.452
want, when each of you flush your toilet, for the stuff that

04:03:04.952 --> 04:03:07.821
you flush to leave your house and not ever come back.

04:03:08.527 --> 04:03:12.683
And we need a sewer treatment plant to be expanded

04:03:12.971 --> 04:03:16.424
so that it can handle what we as a city have. Thank you. We're at

04:03:16.472 --> 04:03:19.674
capacity for load, and we need to do something

04:03:19.788 --> 04:03:23.345
about it now. And it takes 3+ years to

04:03:23.845 --> 04:03:27.803
get a sewer treatment plant built and expanded, and it costs a crap

04:03:28.303 --> 04:03:31.698
ton of money. So that's a huge capital project that's important

04:03:32.198 --> 04:03:35.558
to me. Crap ton of money. Pun intended.

04:03:35.735 --> 04:03:39.104
Oh my gosh, you guys are so quick. I didn't even

04:03:39.120 --> 04:03:42.544
think of that. And then the last

04:03:42.881 --> 04:03:46.026
of the top 3, but there's like about 40 that are important to me,

04:03:46.731 --> 04:03:50.582
are our water sources. The reality

04:03:51.082 --> 04:03:53.887
of it is, is we have a ton of water and a really nice aquifer

04:03:53.967 --> 04:03:57.625
that's under the ground, but we have woefully

04:03:57.737 --> 04:04:00.978
inadequate infrastructure to get that water that

04:04:01.026 --> 04:04:04.812
we have up out of the ground, and we don't

04:04:05.312 --> 04:04:09.290
have enough tanks to store it in. And just to—

04:04:09.932 --> 04:04:13.545
it was the 4th of July week when we put out the request to

04:04:13.690 --> 04:04:17.222
all of our commercial businesses, all of our schools, all of our

04:04:17.722 --> 04:04:21.011
HOAs, all of our churches. And we said, please don't water.

04:04:21.798 --> 04:04:25.218
We said to our residents, please don't water. And we

04:04:25.347 --> 04:04:28.799
as a city had already not been watering our parks. So why is that important?

04:04:28.879 --> 04:04:31.785
Why do we need the whole water infrastructure?

04:04:33.359 --> 04:04:36.618
If we don't have enough water in our tanks replenished at

04:04:36.667 --> 04:04:39.830
night, then we can't put fires out. And we

04:04:39.862 --> 04:04:44.261
live in like a super wildfire-prone

04:04:44.518 --> 04:04:47.810
area. And I don't

04:04:48.310 --> 04:04:51.278
want any of the people in our community to lose their house because we couldn't

04:04:51.778 --> 04:04:55.629
put their fire out because we didn't have enough infrastructure to get the water out

04:04:56.129 --> 04:04:59.931
of the ground. Thank you. And we're working like

04:05:00.431 --> 04:05:04.279
crazy. Our staff is working like crazy. A couple months ago,

04:05:04.904 --> 04:05:10.423
there was a water line that just like burst in the middle of behind

04:05:10.456 --> 04:05:13.632
Scenic Mountain up there, and there were dudes

04:05:14.306 --> 04:05:18.028
from our team working late into the night, frankly early into

04:05:18.076 --> 04:05:21.445
the morning, and it was muddy and gross. And my husband and

04:05:21.945 --> 04:05:24.850
I went and brought them drinks and a couple candy bars. Because I felt bad

04:05:24.899 --> 04:05:29.519
that they were working so late. And it was treacherous

04:05:29.648 --> 04:05:32.932
for me to walk to bring them stuff. I can't imagine what it was like

04:05:33.432 --> 04:05:36.762
for them to work in those conditions. And that's what the people of our

04:05:37.409 --> 04:05:42.585
city staff do over and over again. And I just— you

04:05:42.633 --> 04:05:46.131
guys elect us because we're the ones that go

04:05:46.631 --> 04:05:49.436
and study things out, and then we bring you back information and we make decisions

04:05:49.546 --> 04:05:53.708
on your behalf. This is some of the information that we have

04:05:53.901 --> 04:05:57.051
and the priorities that we have based off of the things that

04:05:57.116 --> 04:06:00.209
you guys have told us. So thank you for being here.

04:06:05.586 --> 04:06:08.511
Can we move to like a policy discussion now?

04:06:08.720 --> 04:06:12.962
Yeah. Okay. Something that's been on my mind is

04:06:13.155 --> 04:06:17.538
how do we prevent this kind of volatility from

04:06:18.038 --> 04:06:21.958
happening in the future. We've benefited. I've benefited personally for the last— I've

04:06:22.458 --> 04:06:26.534
lived here 20 years and my property tax rate has decreased

04:06:27.034 --> 04:06:30.581
as my home value has gone up. I've paid the same nominal dollar

04:06:31.081 --> 04:06:35.382
amount. If you don't believe me, go look at your property tax rate—

04:06:35.398 --> 04:06:38.962
not the rate, excuse me, the dollar amount for the city

04:06:39.701 --> 04:06:42.177
has been the same. Just look at the city.

04:06:43.481 --> 04:06:46.218
I didn't believe it when I served on city council. I'm like, there is no

04:06:46.235 --> 04:06:49.825
way. I was told that. I went home, pulled out all my tax records,

04:06:50.180 --> 04:06:53.413
went back to 2016, pulled out my property tax

04:06:53.913 --> 04:06:57.100
rate. It's like $200. What I paid last year is like $210.

04:06:57.776 --> 04:07:01.270
The way that property taxes work, it's a flat nominal dollar amount,

04:07:01.624 --> 04:07:06.544
but that rate, as your property value is going up, you're paying the same dollar

04:07:07.044 --> 04:07:10.497
amount. So our rate has actually been going down, and you can shake

04:07:10.997 --> 04:07:14.241
your head no. ASD has been raising,

04:07:15.639 --> 04:07:18.347
UFA has been raising, but the city has not been raising.

04:07:19.202 --> 04:07:22.720
So everyone listening, go home, pull out your property

04:07:23.220 --> 04:07:26.956
taxes, look at the city dollar amount that you've paid. It's about

04:07:27.456 --> 04:07:31.147
the same. It's not changed. But the cost

04:07:31.228 --> 04:07:34.571
of the sheriff per Protecting your house, that cost has

04:07:35.171 --> 04:07:38.726
increased. So where we have failed residents,

04:07:38.742 --> 04:07:42.356
and this isn't your fault, it's prior decisions that were made

04:07:42.856 --> 04:07:45.604
that maybe they felt like yeah, it's okay. Data centers will come one day,

04:07:45.994 --> 04:07:49.344
and then all of our problems will be solved. The mayor

04:07:49.844 --> 04:07:52.011
just talked about. I've heard him say that before.

04:07:53.280 --> 04:07:57.222
Whenever that day comes, it's not today. We're not there. So.

04:07:57.926 --> 04:08:02.922
A couple things that I've thought about, and I would be

04:08:03.422 --> 04:08:06.858
curious— this isn't specifically to the rate, but I would

04:08:06.939 --> 04:08:10.029
propose that we have a code, and maybe that's something that we can work on,

04:08:10.706 --> 04:08:13.106
that requires future city councils each,

04:08:13.702 --> 04:08:17.004
whatever that proportion is, whether it's 100% or 50%

04:08:17.101 --> 04:08:20.460
or 60% or 10%, whatever we're

04:08:20.719 --> 04:08:24.420
looking to kind of set that rate at to cover the

04:08:24.920 --> 04:08:28.340
sheriff's contract, I would propose that we have a code that requires

04:08:28.935 --> 04:08:31.762
future city councils to come back every single year,

04:08:32.373 --> 04:08:35.201
review what that— where the council is at.

04:08:35.988 --> 04:08:39.764
If QTS comes online and some of these data

04:08:40.264 --> 04:08:43.926
centers are— our property taxes exceed the sheriff's

04:08:43.974 --> 04:08:47.653
contract, I would like to see our rate go down, and I would like

04:08:48.153 --> 04:08:51.781
to pull that pressure away from the rest But that will only happen

04:08:51.974 --> 04:08:55.972
if we have a code in place, a policy strategically

04:08:56.068 --> 04:09:00.018
long-term that requires councils to come back and review that

04:09:00.518 --> 04:09:03.775
on an annual basis. And I would just be curious if the other

04:09:03.872 --> 04:09:07.404
council— we don't need to vote on that tonight, but I'd be curious

04:09:07.904 --> 04:09:11.226
if you would support moving forward in that direction. Marcus,

04:09:11.338 --> 04:09:15.336
if that is something from a legal standpoint that we can Put

04:09:15.836 --> 04:09:19.305
into our city code? Yeah, I mean, you can put it

04:09:19.805 --> 04:09:22.454
in the code that as part of the budget process, you're gonna review this,

04:09:22.728 --> 04:09:25.941
and the future city councils will either keep that code and follow

04:09:25.957 --> 04:09:29.540
it, or they may change the code at a future date, but then they would

04:09:29.974 --> 04:09:33.654
go through the public process to do that. I know for me, I would've

04:09:34.154 --> 04:09:38.378
been grateful if that existed, because we would've had gradual increases

04:09:39.278 --> 04:09:42.618
year to year. So, so that's

04:09:43.147 --> 04:09:47.463
one comment I have on the policy. I'd also, uh, be— and

04:09:47.543 --> 04:09:50.848
this is more of a legal question for Marcus— the ability for us to

04:09:51.348 --> 04:09:56.431
segregate these funds. Um, we're, we're proposing collecting

04:09:56.463 --> 04:10:00.634
property taxes, that goes into a general fund. Can those be segregated

04:10:01.134 --> 04:10:04.436
by the city into a fund account that just pays specifically for,

04:10:04.966 --> 04:10:09.379
uh, Sheriff's Office? Oh, I think he really wants to I don't

04:10:09.879 --> 04:10:12.013
want to speak, but I can speak to that matter.

04:10:13.072 --> 04:10:16.444
The property taxes will have to be received into the general

04:10:16.944 --> 04:10:20.458
fund. At that point, you could— you can still create a

04:10:20.958 --> 04:10:24.617
separate fund for public safety, but you would do an operating transfer to send

04:10:25.117 --> 04:10:28.085
the revenue over. Would we need a code to require that? It would be a

04:10:28.585 --> 04:10:31.842
resolution that we would bring. So this for 2027,

04:10:31.859 --> 04:10:36.054
there were— We created a new special revenue fund for public

04:10:36.119 --> 04:10:39.625
art and several of those. So we could do that

04:10:40.029 --> 04:10:42.707
specifically for the sheriff's contract? Yeah. Okay, thank you.

04:10:44.270 --> 04:10:47.967
Yeah, I'm glad you brought that up. That's actually one of the— I talked about

04:10:48.581 --> 04:10:52.311
being willing to reduce the property tax as the sales tax

04:10:52.924 --> 04:10:56.746
and the MET money starts to come in commensurate

04:10:57.246 --> 04:11:01.997
so that that burden doesn't stay. I'm definitely in favor of finding

04:11:02.174 --> 04:11:05.547
a solution to that. I also feel pretty strongly

04:11:05.563 --> 04:11:08.403
about making sure that we, if we're saying that we're going to put it to

04:11:08.903 --> 04:11:12.326
public safety, that there should be something that demonstrates to the citizens

04:11:12.423 --> 04:11:15.990
that that's exactly where that's going. So I'm glad that you said that,

04:11:16.055 --> 04:11:19.039
Kimberly. That was one of my questions as well. I think that that needs to

04:11:19.089 --> 04:11:22.357
be clear. I think that we

04:11:22.857 --> 04:11:26.290
need to do our budgets differently. differently, and maybe that's something we need to have

04:11:26.790 --> 04:11:30.528
a discussion about at a later date. One of

04:11:31.028 --> 04:11:32.937
the things that I feel like that we could have done with this, where we're

04:11:33.437 --> 04:11:36.582
proposing a tax increase, I believe we should have had

04:11:36.967 --> 04:11:41.367
our current tax rate budget and a separate budget

04:11:41.463 --> 04:11:44.321
that is based on what the increase would be so that we could actually have

04:11:44.353 --> 04:11:48.753
the very conversation that you guys are asking about. Where would we spend the additional

04:11:49.253 --> 04:11:53.000
funds that are being moved from the sales tax. I'll give you my

04:11:53.500 --> 04:11:56.722
list of things that I think are important. I'm glad the person asked the 3

04:11:57.222 --> 04:12:00.561
top capital projects. In our meeting Tuesday, we talked about

04:12:01.061 --> 04:12:04.846
our master transportation plan. That would be pretty sobering for you if you guys were

04:12:04.862 --> 04:12:08.662
able to see what that cost actually is. What's even more sobering is when

04:12:09.162 --> 04:12:12.978
we sat in a regional meeting to listen to UDOT and the

04:12:13.478 --> 04:12:15.956
Mountainland Association of Governments tell us that we're not going to get some of these

04:12:15.972 --> 04:12:18.423
roads for 10 or 20 years. Council Member Wood,

04:12:18.487 --> 04:12:22.404
$419 million by 2035.

04:12:25.674 --> 04:12:27.640
That's Eagle Mountain's portion. That's ours.

04:12:30.563 --> 04:12:34.630
The only way that that can be paid for is by bonding or leveraging

04:12:34.646 --> 04:12:36.768
debt. We don't have that kind of money.

04:12:37.781 --> 04:12:40.688
We have that kind of need. We're still growing.

04:12:41.042 --> 04:12:44.156
That's not going to change. We still have that many cars that are being added

04:12:44.188 --> 04:12:47.447
every single year. We're being ignored largely

04:12:47.463 --> 04:12:50.705
by the state, frankly, about what our needs are.

04:12:50.914 --> 04:12:55.135
We're absolutely being ignored by the other side of the county as

04:12:55.167 --> 04:12:59.277
they prioritize rapid bus that

04:12:59.777 --> 04:13:03.402
are empty, that we all paid for, by the way, with our UTA funds.

04:13:04.798 --> 04:13:08.250
When they're building gondolas in Salt Lake County, that should have been funds that should

04:13:08.750 --> 04:13:11.388
have come out here and had 2100 North done 4 years ago.

04:13:13.860 --> 04:13:16.796
I want you to know that the fight is happening with who you have right

04:13:17.296 --> 04:13:20.587
here, and whether you decide to vote us in honestly is really immaterial to me.

04:13:21.555 --> 04:13:24.379
If I, if I can't gain your trust by telling you the truth when the

04:13:24.879 --> 04:13:28.060
truth sucks, then you need to go on to somebody else. But I'm going to

04:13:28.076 --> 04:13:31.160
tell you the one thing you can trust is that I will always tell you

04:13:31.660 --> 04:13:34.706
what the truth is, whether you like it or not. And I think I've been

04:13:35.020 --> 04:13:38.327
pretty consistent since I started on the Planning Commission with that.

04:13:38.825 --> 04:13:42.117
I think every developer will tell you that. I'm, I'm about

04:13:42.617 --> 04:13:45.569
what is best for Eagle Mountain as a whole, and I've fought for that.

04:13:46.532 --> 04:13:49.840
When we take a look at policies, policies should be things that outlast us.

04:13:49.920 --> 04:13:53.019
If we don't do things that are going to benefit the people in the future,

04:13:53.420 --> 04:13:55.797
and I do something that is just going to take care of right now,

04:13:56.086 --> 04:13:59.650
then I failed. If we don't put a financial plan

04:13:59.699 --> 04:14:03.165
in place that you guys can rely on, that's a 5 and a 10 and

04:14:03.665 --> 04:14:08.074
a 20-year plan that every council should have to come back and review based

04:14:08.122 --> 04:14:10.256
on what the current needs are at the time, then we failed.

04:14:11.555 --> 04:14:14.619
And I don't like to fail, frankly. I feel like that,

04:14:14.699 --> 04:14:17.892
you know, I had some pretty specific things I wanted to accomplish being on the

04:14:18.392 --> 04:14:21.903
council. I've hit a lot of them. There's a couple of them I'm pretty pissed

04:14:22.403 --> 04:14:25.609
I still haven't hit them. One of them is for sure making sure that

04:14:26.109 --> 04:14:29.670
we're financially sound. And our economic growth has not happened

04:14:29.783 --> 04:14:32.609
at the rate that I would like it to. And I'm tired of being told

04:14:32.657 --> 04:14:35.402
by people that that's the rate of government. I just don't believe that crap.

04:14:36.205 --> 04:14:39.417
I think that there's ways that we can do better. So when

04:14:39.917 --> 04:14:43.045
we vote on this today, my vote's going to be based on that. What is

04:14:43.110 --> 04:14:46.578
best for you in the future? I'm sorry that it's going to

04:14:46.626 --> 04:14:49.500
have the same crappy effect on you that it's having on me and my family.

04:14:49.934 --> 04:14:51.700
My wife is not in favor of it, just so you know.

04:14:53.638 --> 04:14:56.293
And it's going to have an impact on me and my kids,

04:14:56.731 --> 04:14:58.447
whether or not they're able to play certain sports,

04:14:58.906 --> 04:15:02.444
etc. But I'm going to tell you,

04:15:02.945 --> 04:15:05.239
the decision that we're going to make is going to give us the ability to

04:15:05.739 --> 04:15:08.509
start moving traffic. It's going to give us the ability to take care of our

04:15:09.009 --> 04:15:11.983
assets. If we let every one of our parks die, guys, it's going to be

04:15:12.483 --> 04:15:16.324
three times the amount to fix them. So that's kind of where I stand

04:15:16.824 --> 04:15:20.562
with that. The the last thing I want to talk about. Capital projects. Uh,

04:15:20.627 --> 04:15:23.682
I'm not firm that all of it should go to capital projects. I really do

04:15:24.182 --> 04:15:26.548
think that we are undermanned. Our,

04:15:28.043 --> 04:15:30.775
our, uh, labor force is woefully too small.

04:15:31.739 --> 04:15:35.211
Uh, and I'm not talking about people that are sitting at desks. I'm talking about

04:15:35.711 --> 04:15:38.473
the crews that fix our streets, our water,

04:15:39.293 --> 04:15:43.006
our parks. I mean, if you guys knew how

04:15:43.506 --> 04:15:45.894
many employees we had at the parks, it'd probably make you mad. I think it's

04:15:46.394 --> 04:15:49.602
like 10, isn't it, full-time employees for 68

04:15:50.102 --> 04:15:53.420
parks? I mean, that's not going to come

04:15:53.920 --> 04:15:56.361
from nowhere. And so when I look at this, I'm like, if I'm going to

04:15:56.474 --> 04:15:59.752
vote for this, it damn sure is not going to be for people getting

04:15:59.784 --> 04:16:03.125
raises at the top end. We're going to start adding bodies so that we can

04:16:03.157 --> 04:16:06.704
tell people we did our very best to improve the quality

04:16:06.720 --> 04:16:09.619
of life and quality of services that you should expect no matter where you live.

04:16:17.342 --> 04:16:20.177
I support both of those policies you're talking about, by the way.

04:16:21.052 --> 04:16:24.373
I wanted to add just a few other things. So the

04:16:24.873 --> 04:16:28.644
city council has been fighting very hard to find additional money outside the city

04:16:28.822 --> 04:16:32.609
for the city. I spent a week of

04:16:32.789 --> 04:16:36.235
my year this year in March with the mayor

04:16:36.267 --> 04:16:40.020
back in Washington, D.C., and we worked very diligently

04:16:40.212 --> 04:16:44.157
to try to secure federal dollars to come back to support projects

04:16:44.657 --> 04:16:47.846
that we have here in Eagle Mountain. To my understanding, it's the first time our

04:16:48.346 --> 04:16:51.518
city has ever sent a delegation to Washington, D.C. It's not going to be the

04:16:52.018 --> 04:16:55.335
last because we're going to keep beating that drum. That's a week away

04:16:55.431 --> 04:16:58.157
from my family. It's a week away from my own job,

04:16:58.895 --> 04:17:02.576
but it is worth it to me to try to figure out other methods

04:17:02.609 --> 04:17:04.130
to pay for the needs that are here.

04:17:06.162 --> 04:17:09.681
I know that Mayor Gray, he was the one that identified some

04:17:10.181 --> 04:17:13.296
potential significant savings by restructuring our relationship with Unified Fire.

04:17:13.778 --> 04:17:17.286
That's something we're going to experience next year. That was completely on him to

04:17:17.560 --> 04:17:21.411
identify that. That could be millions in savings for the city. And I'm

04:17:21.588 --> 04:17:23.667
grateful for his leadership in that regard.

04:17:26.001 --> 04:17:29.329
We've— other things we're trying to do. We have a volunteer

04:17:29.829 --> 04:17:33.742
police force that also helps alleviate pressure

04:17:34.017 --> 04:17:36.749
on our sheriff's department. They have no funding.

04:17:39.192 --> 04:17:42.517
And Cedar Fort has a fire department that's volunteer, and they don't have enough

04:17:42.565 --> 04:17:46.164
funding. So Alex Carter, who's a council

04:17:46.664 --> 04:17:50.640
member there in Cedar Fort, and I have put our heads together. We're putting

04:17:50.689 --> 04:17:53.938
on a half marathon here in Cedar Valley, October 10th.

04:17:54.553 --> 04:17:58.470
With money that will be raised to help our volunteer police and

04:17:58.486 --> 04:18:01.986
the volunteer fire. We're trying to come up with ways

04:18:02.098 --> 04:18:05.758
to get money from other sources, even small ways.

04:18:06.497 --> 04:18:09.755
We're going to be working with the Best Friends Animal Society where

04:18:10.255 --> 04:18:13.383
they can provide some free services to help with some animal control stuff. We're adding

04:18:13.416 --> 04:18:17.204
those things in there. We're trying to be active, proactive, and we're trying to approach

04:18:17.252 --> 04:18:20.704
this from the front, not the back. Yet we are actively engaged

04:18:21.204 --> 04:18:25.558
with our state, like Legislature, there was a real potential for the

04:18:26.058 --> 04:18:29.086
state to pass a cap on property tax increase.

04:18:29.927 --> 04:18:32.636
Had that passed, we would have been in a very difficult scenario,

04:18:33.391 --> 04:18:36.911
very, very difficult scenario. And and

04:18:37.411 --> 04:18:41.032
I'm glad it didn't. Can we phase this next tax increase?

04:18:42.230 --> 04:18:45.304
Part of the issue is what's the state legislature going to do,

04:18:46.292 --> 04:18:48.784
and we have to consider that and play from the front instead of the back.

04:18:51.374 --> 04:18:54.505
Also, the state legislature has been mentioned that they want to socialize our MET

04:18:54.521 --> 04:18:57.572
funds, our municipal energy tax from data centers. That's not fair.

04:18:58.150 --> 04:19:01.682
So you need a you need a city council that's going to be lobbying very

04:19:02.132 --> 04:19:05.905
very much for you at the state and with the feds to make

04:19:06.405 --> 04:19:09.951
sure we get the best results for you. It was also stated that

04:19:10.015 --> 04:19:14.078
perhaps the tax increase was to give the city council staff pay increases.

04:19:15.207 --> 04:19:19.707
The city council has no staff. Other cities have

04:19:20.207 --> 04:19:22.452
city councils with staff. We don't have a staff. It's us.

04:19:22.614 --> 04:19:24.713
That's it.

04:19:27.633 --> 04:19:30.157
It's been said that we're raising taxes to build a city hall,

04:19:30.848 --> 04:19:33.871
and that's false. This has nothing to do with the city hall.

04:19:34.385 --> 04:19:37.752
Does the city need a city hall? Yes. A lot of— there was comment saying

04:19:38.030 --> 04:19:41.567
you don't need a city hall, you have sufficient here. That's that's an uninformed

04:19:42.067 --> 04:19:45.966
decision or uninformed comment, and I would welcome a private discussion with

04:19:46.466 --> 04:19:49.129
any of you that feel that that that this building is sufficient.

04:19:50.109 --> 04:19:55.118
So I just wanted to clear up some of those things. I think oh

04:19:55.618 --> 04:19:58.956
rec center. This has absolutely nothing to do with the rec center, and hopefully sheds

04:19:59.052 --> 04:20:00.963
light on why we can't do a rec center yet.

04:20:02.280 --> 04:20:04.785
We have so much going on. Let me see. I'm just trying to see.

04:20:06.104 --> 04:20:08.522
The other thing I was going to mention is I'm going to tell you the

04:20:09.022 --> 04:20:12.683
tale of 2 HOAs because it's been brought up. Well, Craig, you're in real estate,

04:20:12.747 --> 04:20:16.503
so you should know that this is not healthy. Let me— it

04:20:17.003 --> 04:20:20.399
is my real estate background that it will cause me to vote yes

04:20:20.608 --> 04:20:23.217
on some form of tax increase right now, and I will tell you why.

04:20:25.212 --> 04:20:28.719
When I was young in my real estate career, actually I wasn't even in real

04:20:28.735 --> 04:20:33.621
estate yet at this moment, I was on a board of an HOA Because

04:20:34.038 --> 04:20:36.859
I was filling in for a period of time with for someone who could not

04:20:37.359 --> 04:20:40.851
be on that board, and that individual said he instructed

04:20:41.351 --> 04:20:45.660
me. He said, "Craig, make sure the HOA dues go up." Like,

04:20:45.740 --> 04:20:48.673
why do you want the HOA dues to go up? This is a business guy.

04:20:49.283 --> 04:20:51.735
Like, why do you want the HOA dues to go up? That's that's the thing

04:20:51.831 --> 04:20:55.037
that will hurt you. You're not going to get money back from that. He said,

04:20:55.053 --> 04:20:58.628
"Craig, I'm planning. I'm not planning on what's going to happen

04:20:58.644 --> 04:21:02.419
to the HOA." this next year. I'm planning at

04:21:02.919 --> 04:21:05.938
a minimum for 5 years, but Craig, I'm actually planning for 10 to 20.

04:21:07.882 --> 04:21:11.246
And so they increased the HOA dues without a need to

04:21:11.279 --> 04:21:14.806
increase the HOA dues. But what did that happen— what,

04:21:14.886 --> 04:21:18.139
what effect did that happen? That HOA has never

04:21:18.639 --> 04:21:22.658
had a special assessment, ever. When roofs came due, they had

04:21:23.158 --> 04:21:25.804
the money for it. When they had to redo parking lots, They had the money

04:21:26.304 --> 04:21:28.405
for it. And when properties go on the market in that HOA,

04:21:29.898 --> 04:21:33.687
they sell in like a day because of how well

04:21:34.187 --> 04:21:37.830
that HOA is maintained. It's because they put— they

04:21:37.846 --> 04:21:41.314
budgeted money early on. What's interesting is there

04:21:41.814 --> 04:21:44.863
is a subdivision immediately next door to them that did not do that.

04:21:45.955 --> 04:21:49.487
It is currently a shuttered facility that will be knocked

04:21:49.987 --> 04:21:53.328
down. That is what will happen. We are at a crossroads.

04:21:54.315 --> 04:21:57.550
I know people have said, well, you are not listening to the people,

04:21:57.566 --> 04:22:00.351
Craig, if you vote for a tax increase because the people don't want it.

04:22:01.115 --> 04:22:04.934
Let me give a brief— this is just a 60-second historical

04:22:05.032 --> 04:22:08.416
moment. The Battle of Gettysburg, there was

04:22:08.513 --> 04:22:12.240
a group that was assigned to protect the flank

04:22:12.304 --> 04:22:13.208
on Little Round Top.

04:22:15.709 --> 04:22:19.314
When the Confederate Army was coming up the hill, that group came in such

04:22:19.330 --> 04:22:22.104
numbers and that they ran out of ammunition.

04:22:23.659 --> 04:22:26.833
The troops said, let us— we got to back out.

04:22:27.076 --> 04:22:28.988
We don't have any more ammunition.

04:22:30.378 --> 04:22:32.612
And their leader said, if we back out now,

04:22:33.535 --> 04:22:36.870
our flank caves in and they will come in behind us and we

04:22:36.951 --> 04:22:40.373
lose the war. And so he told his troops

04:22:40.873 --> 04:22:42.537
that were with him, that were worn and tired,

04:22:44.220 --> 04:22:47.683
put on your bayonets. And they did,

04:22:48.071 --> 04:22:49.091
and they charged down the hill.

04:22:51.898 --> 04:22:55.247
That changed the outcome of the Battle of Gettysburg.

04:22:56.590 --> 04:23:00.026
Sometimes leadership requires leading people to a place they do

04:23:00.526 --> 04:23:03.885
not want to go because we see a vision that we know

04:23:04.063 --> 04:23:08.048
we must obtain. That's what we bring forward to you today that I

04:23:08.129 --> 04:23:10.904
ask that you humbly please at least acknowledge to a degree. Thank you.

04:23:18.459 --> 04:23:22.066
One question that came up when we were in our retreat and different

04:23:22.566 --> 04:23:26.875
discussions through this is, can we create a tax change and

04:23:26.955 --> 04:23:30.481
just create it for like a year or 2 and then roll it

04:23:30.981 --> 04:23:34.088
back? And the fact is state law doesn't allow you to

04:23:34.588 --> 04:23:37.422
do that, but it was something that we as a council were like, yeah,

04:23:37.470 --> 04:23:40.893
we could get behind in that, and it's kind of unfortunate that state law doesn't

04:23:40.909 --> 04:23:44.993
allow you to do that because there will be additional income coming

04:23:45.009 --> 04:23:49.081
into our community soon. But right now we

04:23:49.113 --> 04:23:52.188
have to find money to make sure we get everything accomplished.

04:23:52.268 --> 04:23:55.568
So I wanted to let you guys know if anybody was wondering, why can't you

04:23:56.068 --> 04:23:59.363
just do it for 2 years or a year? It's just not allowed in the

04:24:08.869 --> 04:24:12.219
So I just want to point out maybe one,

04:24:12.429 --> 04:24:15.827
one additional benchmarking tool if it's helpful.

04:24:15.892 --> 04:24:19.443
We, we currently as a city, we are the 9th

04:24:19.508 --> 04:24:22.984
fastest growing city in America, not in Utah,

04:24:23.727 --> 04:24:27.383
but in the United States. Saratoga Springs, I don't know how they beat us.

04:24:27.570 --> 04:24:30.497
It doesn't feel like they did. 20% faster.

04:24:32.485 --> 04:24:35.718
Not that it's a competition. I don't want to be the fastest,

04:24:36.122 --> 04:24:39.749
but we are. Our rate today,

04:24:40.038 --> 04:24:44.730
we are the 2nd lowest in all

04:24:44.778 --> 04:24:48.040
of Utah County behind Cedar Fort. So our current rate is

04:24:48.153 --> 04:24:50.687
0.000534.

04:24:51.477 --> 04:24:54.979
Our— I'll say sister city, Saratoga Springs,

04:24:56.020 --> 04:25:01.205
Maybe they've done a better job of managing that rate, but they're at 0.00976.

04:25:02.159 --> 04:25:06.094
They are also proposing a 100% increase

04:25:06.142 --> 04:25:10.088
to their rate. And I thought about that and I thought Saratoga

04:25:10.588 --> 04:25:14.798
Springs has a much, much larger producing commercial

04:25:14.830 --> 04:25:18.080
base than we do. They have Costco, Home Depot.

04:25:18.549 --> 04:25:22.498
I mean, a lot of our residents shop there. We're actually helping

04:25:22.530 --> 04:25:25.728
them with their revenue. Every time you

04:25:26.228 --> 04:25:29.714
shop at Costco, you're helping Saratoga Springs. When we shop here,

04:25:30.874 --> 04:25:35.430
that helps us from a sales tax point. If this

04:25:35.559 --> 04:25:38.844
proposed rate— and I will just say I am not

04:25:39.344 --> 04:25:42.070
in support of this rate as proposed.

04:25:44.057 --> 04:25:47.454
I think it needs to be lower. It needs to be certainly somewhere in

04:25:47.954 --> 04:25:49.995
the middle. If we increase,

04:25:51.727 --> 04:25:53.387
if we change our rate from 0.0005,

04:25:55.242 --> 04:25:59.016
and there's some other numbers after that, but that'll suffice for

04:25:59.516 --> 04:26:04.047
my point I'm gonna make. If we just doubled our rate to 0.0011%,

04:26:05.330 --> 04:26:09.513
that would, new revenue generated

04:26:09.530 --> 04:26:13.294
would be $3.4 million. So— To be fair to

04:26:13.794 --> 04:26:17.357
our conversation, all of those dollars would go to the sheriff's office.

04:26:18.738 --> 04:26:23.217
That would free up about roughly just under $2 million of

04:26:23.410 --> 04:26:27.023
sales tax revenue that's currently supporting that contract.

04:26:27.729 --> 04:26:31.342
I think at a bare minimum, given all the road infrastructure

04:26:31.842 --> 04:26:35.228
we have, the staffing, getting our employees just to

04:26:36.271 --> 04:26:40.399
90% of the median, That would be the

04:26:40.899 --> 04:26:44.320
lowest that I would consider. But after this

04:26:46.140 --> 04:26:49.875
increase, Saratoga Springs— and again, I'm just kind of benchmarking

04:26:50.085 --> 04:26:53.336
because they are close to us, but I do think they have a better commercial

04:26:53.368 --> 04:26:57.130
base than we do, although we're working on it as

04:26:57.146 --> 04:27:00.506
hard as we can. We have an economic director that is traveling all over

04:27:00.538 --> 04:27:04.076
the country, beating on every— knocking on every door, beating every bush.

04:27:04.190 --> 04:27:08.086
I get emails from her Every it feels like every other day of who

04:27:08.586 --> 04:27:11.691
she's meeting with, who she's talking to, to try to build

04:27:11.755 --> 04:27:15.489
out our commercial base. But with with the proposed increase,

04:27:15.537 --> 04:27:19.174
Saratoga Springs would land at about point zero zero one five,

